 So, if you hit the next slide, we're gonna get into

00:10:09.360 --> 00:10:12.120
 what are these things we actually make?

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:15.720
 In a lot of ways, to me, a newspaper where I've worked

00:10:15.720 --> 00:10:18.400
 at the LA Times for 13 years now and seen a lot of change,

00:10:18.400 --> 00:10:20.800
 but even though we're making different things

00:10:20.800 --> 00:10:24.480
 and different products, it is kind of an information factory.

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:27.080
 So, in a certain sense, a news organization

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:31.400
 takes the raw materials of life, of things

00:10:31.400 --> 00:10:33.040
 that are happening in the world,

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:36.320
 and it refines those raw materials into products,

00:10:36.320 --> 00:10:38.280
 news products that you can sell,

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:41.240
 and the people want that help them make sense of the world.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:46.200
 And traditionally, in my painful metaphor,

00:10:46.200 --> 00:10:48.680
 the assembly lines at this factory were really focused

00:10:48.680 --> 00:10:53.680
 on making a print newspaper that had blocks of text, right?

00:10:54.920 --> 00:10:58.080
 With headlines and photos, and that was like,

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:00.800
 what the majority of the assembly lines were set up to do.

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:03.640
 There were other ones to have sports scores and boxes

00:11:03.640 --> 00:11:05.000
 and to give you the movie listings

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:06.360
 and all these other things.

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:09.200
 But in the rewiring of our economy,

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:11.960
 that's brought on by the internet and all that,

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:15.160
 the factory of the LA Times has to be refitted

00:11:15.160 --> 00:11:18.560
 with new assembly lines to make different products, right?

00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:21.000
 That are more in line with what people want

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:23.760
 that can compete with other factories out there

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:27.280
 in the marketplace, and that can help us shift

00:11:27.280 --> 00:11:31.120
 to a different type of business,

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:34.640
 which is more focused on getting money from subscriptions

00:11:34.640 --> 00:11:36.720
 and readers who like us enough to pay us

00:11:36.720 --> 00:11:38.840
 and less so on advertising,

00:11:38.840 --> 00:11:41.040
 which if you guys are interested in all that business stuff,

00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:43.040
 we can talk about it later, but I won't be too boring.

00:11:43.040 --> 00:11:47.720
 And in our little department, it's my view that our assembly,

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:49.800
 we basically have three assembly lines

00:11:49.800 --> 00:11:52.960
 that the people you saw before kind of interchangeably work

00:11:52.960 --> 00:11:53.800
 on.

00:11:53.800 --> 00:11:58.800
 The first one makes applications, software websites, right?

00:11:59.280 --> 00:12:02.000
 The second makes visual stories.

00:12:02.000 --> 00:12:03.400
 These are stand up, we'll get to those.

00:12:03.400 --> 00:12:06.680
 And then the third is making digital designs.

00:12:06.680 --> 00:12:08.600
 And so let's go through those one, two, three,

00:12:08.600 --> 00:12:10.920
 and I'll show you a bunch of examples of each.

00:12:10.920 --> 00:12:13.520
 So first up is applications.

00:12:13.520 --> 00:12:16.280
 This is where we use our software skills

00:12:16.280 --> 00:12:19.440
 to gather and refine data to serve our digital audience,

00:12:19.440 --> 00:12:21.680
 writing computer code to go get data,

00:12:21.680 --> 00:12:23.960
