00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:53.880
 by the USGS, it sends out like a data feed

00:13:53.880 --> 00:13:56.120
 to the whole world, like kind of little pulses

00:13:56.120 --> 00:13:57.880
 that say here's the latest earthquake

00:13:57.880 --> 00:13:59.920
 and how big it was and where it was.

00:13:59.920 --> 00:14:03.440
 And we have an algorithm that reads every one of those

00:14:03.440 --> 00:14:05.840
 based on how close they are to Los Angeles

00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:07.320
 and how high the magnitude is.

00:14:07.320 --> 00:14:10.160
 We have some sort of editorial barriers.

00:14:10.160 --> 00:14:12.520
 And then if the data surpasses those barriers,

00:14:12.520 --> 00:14:14.760
 this blog post is automatically written

00:14:14.760 --> 00:14:16.560
 and this map is automatically made

00:14:16.560 --> 00:14:18.160
 and prepared for publication.

00:14:18.160 --> 00:14:21.640
 And so within a minute of an earthquake happening,

00:14:21.640 --> 00:14:23.040
 we can have the complete post,

00:14:23.040 --> 00:14:25.640
 including the map ready to roll.

00:14:25.640 --> 00:14:27.520
 We still have a human look at it, of course,

00:14:27.520 --> 00:14:28.920
 before it goes live,

00:14:28.920 --> 00:14:30.600
 but this is where automation gives us

00:14:30.600 --> 00:14:33.760
 kind of a quick step out the door immediately.

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:38.520
 And then the next one would be, I think,

00:14:38.520 --> 00:14:42.320
 our most recent police shootings database,

00:14:42.320 --> 00:14:44.880
 we have a long running project called the homicide report

00:14:44.880 --> 00:14:46.480
 where we have built from the ground up

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:49.000
 our own independent database of every person

00:14:49.000 --> 00:14:52.560
 killed by another in LA County since the year 2000.

00:14:52.560 --> 00:14:55.480
 And this year we decided to repurpose a slice of that data

00:14:55.480 --> 00:14:58.520
 to be a standalone database that just tracks police shootings

00:14:58.520 --> 00:15:01.280
 and gives people a sense of the latest trends

00:15:01.280 --> 00:15:03.200
 and that now, like the homicide report,

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:05.640
 live updates every time we get new records in.

00:15:07.600 --> 00:15:11.080
 These databases, they power these standalone applications

00:15:11.080 --> 00:15:13.840
 and they would probably worth doing just for that reason,

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:16.000
 but they also have the side benefit

00:15:16.000 --> 00:15:19.480
 of allowing us to do enterprise analysis

00:15:19.480 --> 00:15:21.840
 into these issues that we otherwise couldn't do

00:15:21.840 --> 00:15:23.400
 if we didn't have the data, right?

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.560
 So on the next slide, you can see,

00:15:25.560 --> 00:15:27.920
 since we've been gathering all this COVID data

00:15:27.920 --> 00:15:28.760
 for months and months,

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:31.520
 we've been able to do tons of stories about COVID

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:33.400
 that are a little more traditional,

00:15:33.400 --> 00:15:38.000
 but that have stronger claims and more insightful analysis

00:15:38.000 --> 00:15:39.680
 because we have this data to draw from.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:43.560
 So one example would be this kind of step back piece

00:15:43.560 --> 00:15:47.000
 that we did after LA County's sort of botched reopening,

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:49.200
 where we were able to use all the data we gathered

00:15:49.200 --> 00:15:53.200
 and other things we pulled in to kind of tell that story

00:15:53.200 --> 00:15:55.240
 of what happened here in LA

00:15:55.240 --> 00:15:58.100
 in a way that wasn't just a data application, right?

00:15:59.280 --> 00:16:01.640
 And then the next one would come from our homicide report

00:16:01.640 --> 00:16:03.080
 database.

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:04.400
 If you guys follow the news,

00:16:04.400 --> 00:16:06.720
 you may have heard about the case of Deshawn Kizzie,

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:08.240
 who was a young man,

00:16:08.240 --> 00:16:10.920
 killed following a bicycle stop in South LA

00:16:10.920 --> 00:16:13.840
 by sheriff's deputies.

00:16:15.040 --> 00:16:16.320
 And when that happened,

00:16:16.320 --> 00:16:18.000
 it sort of raised a question of,

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:20.600
 well, how common are these bicycle stops

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:22.960
 that lead to fatal encounters?

00:16:22.960 --> 00:16:25.320
 And because we had gathered this database

00:16:25.320 --> 00:16:26.520
 and had all this information,

00:16:26.520 --> 00:16:29.680
 we were the only media outlet that was able to give

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:33.080
 the public the news that, hey, this isn't a loan incident.

00:16:33.080 --> 00:16:35.640
 This is something that's happened 15 times

00:16:35.640 --> 00:16:37.200
 in the last 15 years.

00:16:37.200 --> 00:16:39.520
 And so it's not especially common,

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:41.920
 but it's more common than this being the first time.

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:45.560
 And you're able to bring context and insight to it.

00:16:45.560 --> 00:16:48.040
 You can see if you see that map fly by really quick

00:16:48.040 --> 00:16:49.880
 that these incidents have been concentrated

00:16:49.880 --> 00:16:51.320
 in certain areas too.

00:16:52.200 --> 00:16:54.520
 We have some public records requests out

00:16:54.520 --> 00:16:57.200
 that I believe will allow us to look into that further

00:16:57.200 --> 00:16:58.040
 in the future.

00:16:59.280 --> 00:17:00.400
 So that's thing one,

00:17:00.400 --> 00:17:03.360
 that's the kind of the applications and the analysis.

00:17:03.360 --> 00:17:06.320
 We have live election results coming on Tuesday.

00:17:06.320 --> 00:17:08.320
 So this is all the flowing data feeds

00:17:08.320 --> 00:17:10.160
 of votes coming in from across America.

00:17:10.160 --> 00:17:12.680
 We'll go into maps and tables and charts

00:17:12.680 --> 00:17:17.040
 to help you kind of make sense of what's going on, right?

00:17:17.040 --> 00:17:19.680
 And so all right, so now we can do thing two.

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:24.040
 So assembly line number two is visual stories

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:25.000
 is what I call them.

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:27.960
 This is kind of an emerging story type

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:30.080
 in the world of data and graphics online

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:32.040
 that nobody really has a good name for.

00:17:32.040 --> 00:17:33.640
 So if anybody here has a better name for it,

00:17:33.640 --> 00:17:34.520
 I'd love to hear it.

00:17:34.520 --> 00:17:35.800
 You can hit the next slide.

00:17:39.160 --> 00:17:43.280
 And so visual stories are when graphics reporter reports

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:46.640
 and designs visual coverage that is substantial enough

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:48.080
 to stand on its own.

00:17:48.080 --> 00:17:49.920
 Traditionally graphics departments

00:17:49.920 --> 00:17:52.200
 had sort of made accompanying graphics.

00:17:52.200 --> 00:17:53.920
 So on the old assembly line,

00:17:53.920 --> 00:17:56.200
 you might be making a front page story

00:17:56.200 --> 00:17:59.320
 and you got to have 1600 words of text

00:17:59.320 --> 00:18:00.640
 and you got to have a headline

00:18:00.640 --> 00:18:02.880
 and you got to have maybe a couple photos.

00:18:02.880 --> 00:18:04.720
 And hey, if you're really going to dress up

00:18:04.720 --> 00:18:06.960
 that front page story, you might get a bar chart

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:08.520
 and go with it, right?

00:18:08.520 --> 00:18:12.040
 And so a lot of graphics departments

00:18:12.040 --> 00:18:14.600
 had been oriented around being a support desk

00:18:14.600 --> 00:18:18.160
 that was producing these sort of supplementary graphics

00:18:18.160 --> 00:18:19.160
 for traditional stories.

00:18:19.160 --> 00:18:20.720
 And we love those graphics.

00:18:20.720 --> 00:18:22.400
 They're a good thing, I like them.

00:18:22.400 --> 00:18:24.720
 But I think we have the potential to do quite a bit more

00:18:24.720 --> 00:18:27.240
 and I think our audience wants something a little more.

00:18:27.240 --> 00:18:30.280
 And so one thing I bet everybody here has noticed

00:18:30.280 --> 00:18:33.320
 reading some of the leading news outlets in America

00:18:33.320 --> 00:18:36.280
 is that they now produce these visual stories

00:18:36.280 --> 00:18:39.480
 that are primarily just a combination of graphics

00:18:39.480 --> 00:18:42.560
 that are big enough that they can just stand on their own, right?

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:44.520
 And that's what I call a visual story.

00:18:45.600 --> 00:18:47.760
 And these are pieces that at most places

00:18:47.760 --> 00:18:51.440
 are reported, pitched and executed primarily

00:18:51.440 --> 00:18:53.280
 by the graphics reporter.

00:18:53.280 --> 00:18:57.800
 It's not a case where a writer of a traditional story

00:18:57.800 --> 00:19:01.280
 comes to the graphics department and asks for a graphic, right?

00:19:01.280 --> 00:19:03.960
 So the entire process of how things kind of come together

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:07.200
 has changed and the graphics reporter

00:19:07.200 --> 00:19:10.040
 is more in the driver's seat, okay?

00:19:10.040 --> 00:19:14.560
 And so go ahead and hit the next slide

00:19:14.560 --> 00:19:15.680
 and we'll look at some examples.

00:19:15.680 --> 00:19:18.520
 So early this year, as I'm sure everyone here knows,

00:19:18.520 --> 00:19:21.720
 Kobe Bryant died in a tragic helicopter accident

00:19:21.720 --> 00:19:25.880
 near Calabasas and immediately we thought

00:19:25.880 --> 00:19:27.960
 there's a visual component to the story.

00:19:27.960 --> 00:19:30.160
 For people to understand what happened,

00:19:30.160 --> 00:19:33.360
 you need to know the path of his helicopter, right?

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:37.520
 And so on day one, it was like a map of where did he crash

00:19:37.520 --> 00:19:38.920
 and it's just like an X.

00:19:38.920 --> 00:19:42.120
 And then between day one and day two,

00:19:42.120 --> 00:19:44.440
 it's like, well, what was the path of the helicopter

00:19:44.440 --> 00:19:48.400
 and what was really going on and what's the play-by-play, right?

00:19:48.400 --> 00:19:49.840
 And then what we kind of learned on,

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:52.560
 it happened on a Sunday, what we kind of learned on Monday

00:19:52.560 --> 00:19:55.960
 is like, oh, well, it seems like the final like part

00:19:55.960 --> 00:19:59.200
 of this helicopter flight is where things really went wrong.

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:01.160
 And this was kind of the crucial moment.

00:20:01.160 --> 00:20:03.200
 And so we decide, well, let's try to do a story

00:20:03.200 --> 00:20:06.880
 that helps people see or understand as best as we can

00:20:06.880 --> 00:20:07.720
 what happened.

00:20:07.720 --> 00:20:11.440
 And so we took the publicly available data about the helicopter

00:20:11.440 --> 00:20:14.440
 and we turned it into like a three or four step

00:20:14.440 --> 00:20:17.560
 little graphic here that has, for each chapter in the story,

00:20:17.560 --> 00:20:20.720
 has like a 3D map that helps you see what's going on.

00:20:20.720 --> 00:20:24.240
 And by the end, you sort of are able to see

00:20:24.240 --> 00:20:27.280
 this sort of important thing where the helicopter

00:20:27.280 --> 00:20:30.000
 began climbing very rapidly in the last few seconds

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:32.280
 before a quick turn and then a crash.

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:34.560
 And that's the sort of thing that you can describe in words

00:20:34.560 --> 00:20:39.560
 but is maybe better understood by seeing a 3D model, right?

00:20:40.840 --> 00:20:43.840
 So the next example on the next slide

00:20:43.840 --> 00:20:46.760
 would be something from politics.

00:20:46.760 --> 00:20:49.760
 So we have this very tight DA race, we think.

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:52.920
 And after the primary, when we knew we were going

00:20:52.920 --> 00:20:55.360
 to run off, we get the precinct level results

00:20:55.360 --> 00:20:58.080
 that show you where people voted in every neighborhood.

00:20:58.080 --> 00:21:00.560
 And we kind of did an analysis that looked at,

00:21:00.560 --> 00:21:02.320
 well, where is the incumbent strong?

00:21:02.320 --> 00:21:04.200
 Where is the challenger strong?

00:21:04.200 --> 00:21:07.320
 And what would an upset coalition have to look like

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:09.080
 based on past election results

00:21:09.080 --> 00:21:10.920
 and what we kind of know about this election?

00:21:10.920 --> 00:21:14.760
 So this piece is really about helping people understand

00:21:14.760 --> 00:21:19.840
 the political calculus of this local race through maps, right?

00:21:19.840 --> 00:21:24.840
 And then the next one is we did a local mask survey.

00:21:26.560 --> 00:21:28.120
 So there was this thing over the summer,

00:21:28.120 --> 00:21:31.240
 the question of how many people are actually really wearing masks

00:21:31.240 --> 00:21:34.360
 and rather than just quote public opinion polls

00:21:34.360 --> 00:21:36.600
 or take data from other places,

00:21:36.600 --> 00:21:38.440
 we decided to conduct our own survey.

00:21:38.440 --> 00:21:41.680
 So a dozen of our reporters went to three different locations

00:21:41.680 --> 00:21:44.080
 of multiple times over a few weeks.

00:21:44.080 --> 00:21:47.040
 We followed a method that Jill Darling at USC

00:21:47.040 --> 00:21:48.760
 helped us like figure out.

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:50.360
 And then we were able to kind of come back

00:21:50.360 --> 00:21:52.440
 with our own provisional conclusions

00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:56.960
 about how many people are wearing masks roughly

00:21:56.960 --> 00:22:00.440
 and the variations between the different locations we looked at.

00:22:00.440 --> 00:22:02.520
 And then that's presented here

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:04.960
 as sort of a designee standalone thing

00:22:04.960 --> 00:22:06.640
 that's really more about charts and maps

00:22:06.640 --> 00:22:08.360
 than it is about text, right?

00:22:09.600 --> 00:22:14.200
 Next slide would be something that's not really data at all.

00:22:14.200 --> 00:22:15.160
 It's more visual.

00:22:15.160 --> 00:22:17.440
 And so in the George Floyd protests,

00:22:17.440 --> 00:22:19.240
 I'm sure everyone saw on social media,

00:22:19.240 --> 00:22:22.640
 there was a lot of footage of the police here in LA

00:22:22.640 --> 00:22:24.920
 being pretty violent.

00:22:24.920 --> 00:22:28.760
 And so we did a piece where we tried to aggregate

00:22:28.760 --> 00:22:30.440
 as much as that as possible

00:22:30.440 --> 00:22:35.280
 and then help compare it to the actual standards

00:22:35.280 --> 00:22:38.120
 for use of force the police are supposed to follow

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:40.160
 to show the contrast.

00:22:40.160 --> 00:22:42.640
 And so this is a case where we're using the design

00:22:42.640 --> 00:22:46.520
 and the visual graph and the videos to tell the story

00:22:46.520 --> 00:22:49.880
 and it's not really like charts and maps, you know what I mean?

00:22:49.880 --> 00:22:51.840
 But it still falls within kind of this

00:22:51.840 --> 00:22:56.320
 under this visual umbrella that I think is part of what we do

00:22:56.320 --> 00:22:58.760
 and it's why I call it a visual story

00:22:58.760 --> 00:23:01.280
 and not a graphic story or something, right?

00:23:02.680 --> 00:23:05.360
 And we have another example I'm trying to remember.

00:23:05.360 --> 00:23:07.360
 Oh, and then here's one we did on homeless housing.

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:10.760
 This is a type of visual story I bet you've all seen too

00:23:10.760 --> 00:23:14.440
 that we call scrolly telling, which is a terrible term,

00:23:14.440 --> 00:23:17.360
 which is where as you scroll things sort of move and happen

00:23:17.360 --> 00:23:19.680
 and this is another type of visual technique

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:22.840
 that we use from time to time, depending on whether it fits

00:23:22.840 --> 00:23:26.000
 the story we're trying to tell, okay?

00:23:26.000 --> 00:23:27.480
 I think that's it for those examples.

00:23:27.480 --> 00:23:29.560
 And then I think we get to things three,

00:23:29.560 --> 00:23:31.960
 if I remember my deck right, which is digital designs.

00:23:31.960 --> 00:23:35.280
 Oh, no, it doesn't fit the screen size.

00:23:35.280 --> 00:23:37.480
 My gosh, you found a bug in my deck,

00:23:37.480 --> 00:23:42.040
 but this is a case where we're using our digital design skills

00:23:42.040 --> 00:23:45.360
 to elevate the newsroom's most ambitious journalism

00:23:45.360 --> 00:23:46.720
 through like what we think

00:23:46.720 --> 00:23:49.720
 are cool digital custom presentations.

00:23:49.720 --> 00:23:53.600
 And in this case, we're usually helping to elevate stories

00:23:53.600 --> 00:23:56.360
 developed by other departments rather than our own work.

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:57.760
 And a lot of it has some, you know,

00:23:57.760 --> 00:23:59.800
 nothing to do with data or graphics.

00:23:59.800 --> 00:24:02.600
 We're kind of the boutique web designers

00:24:02.600 --> 00:24:06.080
 for these like flagship projects of the newsroom, okay?

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:08.680
 So examples of that wouldn't be on the following slide.

00:24:08.680 --> 00:24:12.280
 So the first one would be we did an obituaries package

00:24:12.280 --> 00:24:15.200
 of all the people who died from COVID.

00:24:17.280 --> 00:24:20.120
 Not all of them of many people who died in COVID,

00:24:20.120 --> 00:24:21.400
 of COVID here locally.

00:24:21.400 --> 00:24:23.800
 And then that package has sort of a custom design

00:24:23.800 --> 00:24:25.840
 to try to make it more dramatic and interesting

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:27.680
 and engaging for the reader.

00:24:27.680 --> 00:24:29.640
 The next example is one from Sunday.

00:24:29.640 --> 00:24:31.160
 I wonder if anybody here saw it.

00:24:31.160 --> 00:24:34.000
 We had a piece by Rosanna Shee about DDT

00:24:34.000 --> 00:24:37.600
 being dumped off the coast back in the day here in LA.

00:24:37.600 --> 00:24:40.760
 And to try to make that story more impactful and grabby

00:24:40.760 --> 00:24:43.400
 and to get people we did custom design for it,

00:24:43.400 --> 00:24:45.560
 which included like a video topper,

00:24:45.560 --> 00:24:48.360
 which you see here, a little scrolly telling in the middle.

00:24:48.360 --> 00:24:52.080
 And then the sort of overall design and theme of the page

00:24:52.080 --> 00:24:54.120
 is customized to fit the story.

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:58.880
 It isn't just the generic LA times kind of look, right?

00:24:58.880 --> 00:25:02.880
 Now the next one is the Chicano moratorium package

00:25:02.880 --> 00:25:04.760
 over the summer, we had an anniversary

00:25:04.760 --> 00:25:08.640
 of a famous protest in East LA against the Vietnam War,

00:25:08.640 --> 00:25:12.000
 which ended up being a series of stories.

00:25:12.000 --> 00:25:13.680
 And in the past, this might have just been

00:25:13.680 --> 00:25:16.360
 like a newspaper special suction,

00:25:16.360 --> 00:25:18.560
 and then a bunch of pages on our website

00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:19.960
 that aren't really connected.

00:25:19.960 --> 00:25:22.960
 But what we try to do is to bring a style and theme

00:25:22.960 --> 00:25:23.840
 to the whole package.

00:25:23.840 --> 00:25:26.040
 This is kind of the cover page where all the stories

00:25:26.040 --> 00:25:28.800
 were collected, and then each of the individual stories

00:25:28.800 --> 00:25:31.160
 was given sort of a variation on the theme

00:25:31.160 --> 00:25:33.120
 that you see here is the idea.

00:25:34.280 --> 00:25:36.440
 And I think next up is the one I'm gonna,

00:25:36.440 --> 00:25:39.280
 oh, then sometimes we do these for other platforms,

00:25:39.280 --> 00:25:40.640
 not just for the open web.

00:25:40.640 --> 00:25:42.720
 The Linux nerd in me would love it all just to be

00:25:42.720 --> 00:25:47.280
 on RWW, but as far as our business goes,

00:25:47.280 --> 00:25:49.280
 we do need to cross publish these things

00:25:49.280 --> 00:25:54.280
 on monopolistic corporate platforms, guys, like Apple News.

00:25:54.280 --> 00:25:57.200
 And so sometimes we will customize stuff

00:25:57.200 --> 00:25:59.720
 to work on those other places as well,

00:25:59.720 --> 00:26:02.120
 as you see here with our voter guide,

00:26:02.120 --> 00:26:05.120
 which ran inside of Apple News.

00:26:06.480 --> 00:26:08.120
 And then we have, I think the next slide

00:26:08.120 --> 00:26:09.760
 will just tease one we have coming,

00:26:09.760 --> 00:26:13.480
 we're gonna try to do a crazier year in review package

00:26:14.680 --> 00:26:16.160
 that will be more visual and fun

00:26:16.160 --> 00:26:18.120
 than what we typically done in the past.

00:26:18.120 --> 00:26:20.560
 So those are the three types of things we do.

00:26:21.560 --> 00:26:24.640
 The next section kind of gets into what that requires

00:26:24.640 --> 00:26:26.720
 for a change, and some of this is a little more

00:26:26.720 --> 00:26:29.480
 in my message for people eternally at the LA Times,

00:26:29.480 --> 00:26:31.040
 but I think it'll work for you guys too,

00:26:31.040 --> 00:26:34.440
 which is for this, for our data and graphics department

00:26:34.440 --> 00:26:38.000
 to make these things and to achieve this mission,

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:41.600
 they have to be more independent and ambitious

00:26:41.600 --> 00:26:44.920
 than maybe a traditional graphics department has been,

00:26:44.920 --> 00:26:46.720
 and they need to have a higher skill level.

00:26:46.720 --> 00:26:50.160
 So that requires us to seek out

00:26:50.160 --> 00:26:52.800
 and develop our own ideas,

00:26:52.800 --> 00:26:55.280
 to look at the major storylines of the day,

00:26:55.280 --> 00:26:58.800
 be it COVID or COB or the Dodgers winning

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:01.560
 or the election or the George Floyd protests

00:27:01.560 --> 00:27:02.880
 or homelessness here locally

00:27:02.880 --> 00:27:04.760
 or whatever those major topics are

00:27:04.760 --> 00:27:08.920
 that we know both matter to our democracy,

00:27:08.920 --> 00:27:12.400
 but also to our audience in terms of what they want to pay for.

00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:14.320
 We need to look at those topics and say,

00:27:14.320 --> 00:27:17.720
 our job is to develop striking visual and data coverage

00:27:17.720 --> 00:27:18.960
 that people are going to want,

00:27:18.960 --> 00:27:20.920
 and that means we have to be more ambitious

00:27:20.920 --> 00:27:22.920
 than some departments have been in the past.

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:24.720
 We need to take more initiative,

00:27:24.720 --> 00:27:27.560
 and we need to operate more like a traditional news department

00:27:27.560 --> 00:27:31.560
 in having our own sort of editing system and people in place.

00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:32.960
 So that's kind of this section.

00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:34.840
 And you can just jump through the next few slides

00:27:34.840 --> 00:27:39.160
 and we'll get to where the next section,

00:27:39.160 --> 00:27:40.680
 I just talk about the results.

00:27:40.680 --> 00:27:42.880
 So just, you can jump ahead here a couple.

00:27:45.720 --> 00:27:48.600
 There's Alex Tatuzzi and my deputy editor, my hero.

00:27:48.600 --> 00:27:50.200
 And then the next slide is our listing.

00:27:50.200 --> 00:27:52.400
 We have a job listing for a second deputy editor

00:27:52.400 --> 00:27:56.160
 as we try to increase our editing muscle on our team,

00:27:56.160 --> 00:27:58.080
 which is one of my major strategic goals

00:27:58.080 --> 00:27:59.240
 for the current moment.

00:28:01.080 --> 00:28:03.360
 And the reason we pursue all these goals

00:28:03.360 --> 00:28:05.920
 is not just 'cause we're nerds and we like to do it,

00:28:05.920 --> 00:28:07.600
 which is maybe a good enough reason

00:28:07.600 --> 00:28:10.360
 if you're not expecting to get paid.

00:28:11.200 --> 00:28:16.280
 But we also are pursuing this different type of journalism.

00:28:16.280 --> 00:28:17.480
 One, because we like doing it.

00:28:17.480 --> 00:28:19.000
 Two, because it's better journalism.

00:28:19.000 --> 00:28:21.720
 The three, because it pays and it's better for our business.

00:28:21.720 --> 00:28:26.720
 And so the next section is really kind of covers

00:28:26.880 --> 00:28:29.200
 that these types of things I've been showing you,

00:28:29.200 --> 00:28:31.960
 they're more popular with audiences.

00:28:31.960 --> 00:28:34.640
 They're more valuable in that they are seen

00:28:34.640 --> 00:28:38.600
 to convert more new subscribers to the digital product,

00:28:38.600 --> 00:28:41.920
 which is our number one business goal than a typical story.

00:28:41.920 --> 00:28:44.040
 And they're more durable in that they continue

00:28:44.040 --> 00:28:47.120
 to draw an audience long after they're initially published,

00:28:47.120 --> 00:28:50.600
 where most new stories kind of peter out within a day or two.

00:28:50.600 --> 00:28:54.040
 So this next section is all redacted,

00:28:54.040 --> 00:28:56.800
 so we don't need to look at it, but I can give you the gist.

00:28:56.800 --> 00:28:58.800
 I mean, you probably aren't gonna be surprised

00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:00.000
 to hear this if you think about it,

00:29:00.000 --> 00:29:02.240
 but our coronavirus tracker,

00:29:02.240 --> 00:29:04.360
 as humble as it may appear to you,

00:29:04.360 --> 00:29:06.560
 is the most popular page in the history

00:29:06.560 --> 00:29:08.120
 of the Los Angeles Times website.

00:29:08.120 --> 00:29:10.840
 No page has ever been visited by more people.

00:29:10.840 --> 00:29:13.360
 And if you compare it to our top traditional stories,

00:29:13.360 --> 00:29:15.680
 it is multiple times more popular

00:29:15.680 --> 00:29:19.020
 than the most popular stories of the year.

00:29:19.020 --> 00:29:21.320
 And then when you look at conversions

00:29:21.320 --> 00:29:25.760
 in terms of what's valuable in attracting new paying subscribers,

00:29:25.760 --> 00:29:30.120
 it is an order of magnitude higher

00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:33.680
 than the number two story of the entire year.

00:29:33.680 --> 00:29:36.800
 And so you see that when things like this connect,

00:29:36.800 --> 00:29:40.560
 they have a sort of home run possibility for the business

00:29:40.560 --> 00:29:44.520
 that a traditional story very rarely can have.

00:29:44.520 --> 00:29:47.120
 And then if you look at that over time,

00:29:47.120 --> 00:29:50.320
 you'll see that it didn't become the top converting story

00:29:50.320 --> 00:29:53.360
 until three or four months after it had initially launched,

00:29:53.360 --> 00:29:56.360
 which just shows that it's continuing to draw audience

00:29:56.360 --> 00:29:59.400
 and new paying customers over time

00:29:59.400 --> 00:30:01.840
 and that sort of durability I was talking about.

00:30:01.840 --> 00:30:04.040
 And so for those reasons,

00:30:04.040 --> 00:30:06.840
 we think that there's a strong business logic

00:30:06.840 --> 00:30:08.520
 behind investing in what we do.

00:30:08.520 --> 00:30:10.960
 And I'll kind of close with this, I guess maybe,

00:30:10.960 --> 00:30:14.360
 but having been a news nerd, as people say,

00:30:14.360 --> 00:30:18.320
 and having done this kind of thing for nearly 20 years now,

00:30:18.320 --> 00:30:19.920
 that has been the biggest change

00:30:19.920 --> 00:30:22.200
 that I have seen in the last five or 10 years.

00:30:22.200 --> 00:30:26.680
 When I began this as like a computer programmer journalist

00:30:26.680 --> 00:30:29.920
 in 2000, we were curiosities.

00:30:29.920 --> 00:30:34.200
 We were either the one person on the investigative team

00:30:34.200 --> 00:30:38.120
 who had learned to code to try to do a cool data analysis

00:30:38.120 --> 00:30:40.680
 for a traditional print investigative piece,

00:30:40.680 --> 00:30:44.000
 or you were some weird person in the corner

00:30:44.000 --> 00:30:46.560
 who said the internet's gonna be big one day,

00:30:46.560 --> 00:30:49.240
 and we need to learn how to make new weird things.

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:51.840
 And so the justifications for most of the teams

00:30:51.840 --> 00:30:54.640
 that I've been on in my entire career

00:30:54.640 --> 00:30:57.480
 were either this is data analysis

00:30:57.480 --> 00:31:00.120
 to do more impactful and important investigative reporting.

00:31:00.120 --> 00:31:02.120
 That's actually what got me into it initially

00:31:02.120 --> 00:31:04.560
 and is still my passion, right?

00:31:04.560 --> 00:31:08.600
 Or it was this is sort of boutique internet experimentation

00:31:08.600 --> 00:31:11.880
 to kind of see what comes of it, right?

00:31:11.880 --> 00:31:16.320
 And we're now far enough into the digital transition

00:31:16.320 --> 00:31:17.920
 to the information economy

00:31:17.920 --> 00:31:19.720
 that that's just fundamentally changed.

00:31:19.720 --> 00:31:21.960
 And what we've seen is that there are certain types

00:31:21.960 --> 00:31:25.160
 of these products, these data and graphics products

00:31:25.160 --> 00:31:26.760
 that are so successful

00:31:26.760 --> 00:31:29.920
 that there's now like a capitalistic economic logic

00:31:29.920 --> 00:31:31.800
 for producing them.

00:31:31.800 --> 00:31:34.000
 And there's less and less of a logic

00:31:34.000 --> 00:31:36.560
 for producing what these departments produced before.

00:31:36.560 --> 00:31:39.040
 And so that's why I think not to get,

00:31:39.040 --> 00:31:40.280
 I guess I am at the university

00:31:40.280 --> 00:31:42.680
 so I can get all Marxist on you guys, you know,

00:31:42.680 --> 00:31:45.960
 like I think that that is this economic logic

00:31:45.960 --> 00:31:48.800
 that is driving kind of the evolution of our team

00:31:48.800 --> 00:31:50.960
 and the justification for growing it,

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:55.120
 as well as similar changes you see in other departments

00:31:55.120 --> 00:31:57.240
 like ours at other news organizations,

00:31:57.240 --> 00:32:01.280
 some of which are like us in the middle of the transition

00:32:01.280 --> 00:32:04.880
 and some others are further along down the road.

00:32:04.880 --> 00:32:07.040
 Like for instance, the New York Times graphics desk

00:32:07.040 --> 00:32:10.160
 which is already fully converted into this type of thing

00:32:10.160 --> 00:32:12.920
 that I've been talking about here today.

00:32:12.920 --> 00:32:15.640
 And I don't work there so I don't know the numbers

00:32:15.640 --> 00:32:18.560
 but I would estimate based on what we see

00:32:18.560 --> 00:32:21.600
 and based upon how the number of things they put out

00:32:21.600 --> 00:32:23.200
 and the quality of what they put out

00:32:23.200 --> 00:32:25.920
 that like the New York Times graphics department

00:32:25.920 --> 00:32:29.360
 may be the most read and most profitable department

00:32:29.360 --> 00:32:31.840
 in the whole newsroom, like that wouldn't surprise me

00:32:31.840 --> 00:32:33.800
 with maybe the exception of one or two people

00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:35.700
 who write about Trump, you know.

00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:41.040
 And that is something that just was not true in the past

00:32:41.040 --> 00:32:44.840
 when these departments were seen as supplementary, right?

00:32:45.760 --> 00:32:47.840
 So that's my spiel.

00:32:47.840 --> 00:32:50.000
 I'm happy to talk about really any of these issues

00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:52.360
 that were raised, any of the stories in particular,

00:32:52.360 --> 00:32:54.320
 any, you guys want to talk about Python?

00:32:54.320 --> 00:32:56.240
 I'll talk D3, I'll talk about that.

00:32:57.240 --> 00:32:58.520
 Whatever you guys want to talk about,

00:32:58.520 --> 00:33:01.480
 I'm happy to just jam and hang out.

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:07.600
 - All right, well, that was fascinating.

00:33:07.600 --> 00:33:10.520
 I do certainly want to open it up to the audience

00:33:10.520 --> 00:33:13.160
 because this is about the students who are here

00:33:13.160 --> 00:33:14.760
 participating this week.

00:33:15.680 --> 00:33:18.840
 Maybe I'll have Andy kind of summarize the questions

00:33:18.840 --> 00:33:21.200
 in the chat room, but in the meantime,

00:33:21.200 --> 00:33:23.880
 well, you know, go ahead and post questions

00:33:23.880 --> 00:33:26.320
 that you have for Ben in the chat room,

00:33:26.320 --> 00:33:29.480
 but maybe I'll kick it off a little bit here, Ben.

00:33:29.480 --> 00:33:32.480
 You really talk about this kind of transformative age

00:33:32.480 --> 00:33:36.880
 of data journalism through this kind of visual evolution.

00:33:38.040 --> 00:33:40.520
 What's just kind of like mind boggling to me

00:33:40.520 --> 00:33:45.120
 is the fluidity by which you guys go about your business.

00:33:45.120 --> 00:33:47.280
 Thinking like any one of the products

00:33:47.280 --> 00:33:50.600
 that you have showcased in your presentation,

00:33:50.600 --> 00:33:54.120
 if we were to do any one of those in academic institution,

00:33:54.120 --> 00:33:56.720
 it'll probably take six months to create.

00:33:56.720 --> 00:34:01.320
 And just kind of, you know, be followed by this,

00:34:01.320 --> 00:34:05.680
 your ability to, you know, pivot so quickly

00:34:05.680 --> 00:34:06.680
 with a new story.

00:34:06.680 --> 00:34:08.720
 And then the next day you have like something

00:34:08.720 --> 00:34:12.320
 that looks like it took six months to create.

00:34:12.320 --> 00:34:15.720
 Can you talk a little bit about this kind of this environment

00:34:15.720 --> 00:34:18.560
 that you work in, the speed that you have to work in?

00:34:18.560 --> 00:34:20.560
 And you ever sleep?

00:34:20.560 --> 00:34:23.040
 - Well, I'm glad you think that during COVID, very rarely,

00:34:23.040 --> 00:34:24.520
 as you can tell by looking at me,

00:34:24.520 --> 00:34:27.720
 but like after the selection,

00:34:27.720 --> 00:34:30.520
 the dream is like December, like maybe a day off,

00:34:30.520 --> 00:34:31.360
 but like,

00:34:33.800 --> 00:34:37.280
 but more seriously, it's really like any,

00:34:37.280 --> 00:34:39.680
 like, you know, I like the factory metaphor,

00:34:39.680 --> 00:34:41.240
 but I'm sure there's other ways to talk about it,

00:34:41.240 --> 00:34:46.240
 but it's like any process, any like manufacturing process,

00:34:46.920 --> 00:34:48.520
 you break it down into pieces

00:34:48.520 --> 00:34:51.360
 and then you like get really good at like, you know,

00:34:51.360 --> 00:34:53.280
 getting each of those pieces of things

00:34:53.280 --> 00:34:55.880
 that need to get done kind of like handled, you know,

00:34:55.880 --> 00:35:00.000
 and you then try to speed up the process as much as you can.

00:35:00.000 --> 00:35:04.640
 And so like in the case of like a visual story, for instance,

00:35:04.640 --> 00:35:07.560
 you can think about it in that abstract manufacturing way

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:10.920
 in the sense that you need a toolkit

00:35:10.920 --> 00:35:14.000
 that lets you stand up a single static page

00:35:14.000 --> 00:35:16.280
 that's outside of like a traditional

00:35:16.280 --> 00:35:19.480
 rigid content management system, right?

00:35:19.480 --> 00:35:22.240
 That can be custom coded and designed.

00:35:22.240 --> 00:35:24.440
 And so what you'll find is that at our department

00:35:24.440 --> 00:35:26.520
 and every other one like it,

00:35:26.520 --> 00:35:28.680
 that people have their little quote framework

00:35:28.680 --> 00:35:31.760
 or their little, you know, publishing rig

00:35:31.760 --> 00:35:36.080
 that lets them spin up a standalone web page.

00:35:36.080 --> 00:35:37.880
 And then within that, there's like components

00:35:37.880 --> 00:35:39.960
 of web development that make it easier for you

00:35:39.960 --> 00:35:42.520
 to move quickly like a template inheritance.

00:35:42.520 --> 00:35:44.280
 Well, within that system,

00:35:44.280 --> 00:35:47.200
 we have a base template that has all the LA time styles

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:49.200
 and fonts and the header and footer

00:35:49.200 --> 00:35:51.160
 and like all the basic pieces.

00:35:51.160 --> 00:35:53.480
 And so then those are just like there for you

00:35:53.480 --> 00:35:56.760
 when you like get the page like rolling, you know what I mean?

00:35:56.760 --> 00:35:59.000
 And you're within that already.

00:35:59.000 --> 00:36:00.560
 And then by the push of a button,

00:36:00.560 --> 00:36:02.040
 you can trigger a little deploy

00:36:02.040 --> 00:36:04.240
 that will send that like blank page live.

00:36:04.240 --> 00:36:08.040
 And then within the framework, you, you know,

00:36:08.040 --> 00:36:09.160
 depending on how you set it up,

00:36:09.160 --> 00:36:11.560
 you have ways to import data files

00:36:11.560 --> 00:36:14.160
 and then to quickly get them into JavaScript

00:36:14.160 --> 00:36:16.240
 and other tools so that you can lay out the page

00:36:16.240 --> 00:36:18.040
 kind of as quickly as possible.

00:36:18.040 --> 00:36:20.240
 And our system isn't as streamlined as it could be

00:36:20.240 --> 00:36:21.480
 or as it should be, you know,

00:36:21.480 --> 00:36:25.240
 but like every, we've developed it ourselves internally

00:36:25.240 --> 00:36:28.000
 and we're trying to make it better like all the time.

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:29.240
 Like this week we were talking about,

00:36:29.240 --> 00:36:33.600
 well, how could we get it better at producing a headless page

00:36:33.600 --> 00:36:35.440
 that's for iframing in other places?

00:36:35.440 --> 00:36:37.320
 You know, how could we make a template

00:36:37.320 --> 00:36:39.680
 that's just like ready to make an iframe page

00:36:39.680 --> 00:36:42.680
 that could go on the homepage in 10 minutes, right?

00:36:42.680 --> 00:36:46.760
 And so you have to have like that toolkit that's there.

00:36:46.760 --> 00:36:48.200
 You have to have someone on staff

00:36:48.200 --> 00:36:50.280
 who can like build and maintain such a toolkit

00:36:50.280 --> 00:36:52.280
 because they don't really exist.

00:36:52.280 --> 00:36:54.680
 One of my goals is for there to be

00:36:54.680 --> 00:36:58.640
 a more standard open source sort of like thing for that.

00:36:58.640 --> 00:36:59.480
 You know what I mean?

00:36:59.480 --> 00:37:01.680
 You look at tool like when we make an application

00:37:01.680 --> 00:37:04.080
 like the live election results or the tracker,

00:37:04.080 --> 00:37:06.160
 we might use Django or some of these

00:37:06.160 --> 00:37:09.640
 pretty highly developed web frameworks for databases.

00:37:09.640 --> 00:37:11.720
 But when it comes to static pages,

00:37:11.720 --> 00:37:15.200
 you really just have kind of like a hodgepodge

00:37:15.200 --> 00:37:17.760
 of like node framework thingies

00:37:17.760 --> 00:37:20.440
 that like can kind of halfway make a page

00:37:20.440 --> 00:37:22.360
 but they don't have the 30 different things

00:37:22.360 --> 00:37:23.920
 you actually need to make a real page

00:37:23.920 --> 00:37:25.720
 that you want to publish, right?

00:37:25.720 --> 00:37:28.720
 And like kind of, I think that there's the possibility

00:37:28.720 --> 00:37:31.240
 for a stronger open source framework

00:37:31.240 --> 00:37:34.040
 that goes beyond just the basic tools

00:37:34.040 --> 00:37:36.560
 and gets into a little more of the opinionated decisions

00:37:36.560 --> 00:37:38.480
 about how to manage deployment

00:37:38.480 --> 00:37:41.720
 and static assets and data files and stuff.

00:37:41.720 --> 00:37:43.440
 Wow, that was way too nerdy.

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:45.760
 But like that's part of it.

00:37:45.760 --> 00:37:47.480
 That's like the technology part.

00:37:47.480 --> 00:37:49.800
 And then there's the kind of editing and journalism part

00:37:49.800 --> 00:37:53.360
 which is identifying either planned events

00:37:53.360 --> 00:37:54.480
 that are ahead on the calendar

00:37:54.480 --> 00:37:56.840
 that you know are coming like the election

00:37:56.840 --> 00:38:00.760
 or pivoting when a major storyline emerges

00:38:00.760 --> 00:38:03.040
 and then focusing your assignment on it.

00:38:03.040 --> 00:38:05.320
 And so like in the case of like the Kobe thing,

00:38:05.320 --> 00:38:08.480
 it's like Kobe's helicopter crashes on Sunday.

00:38:08.480 --> 00:38:10.440
 In a breaking news situation like that,

00:38:10.440 --> 00:38:14.280
 we tend to think in terms of like rounds of coverage

00:38:14.280 --> 00:38:15.120
 or stages.

00:38:15.120 --> 00:38:16.680
 So on day one, we're going to do this.

00:38:16.680 --> 00:38:18.360
 On day two, we're going to do this.

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:19.640
 And then we're going to aim for something

00:38:19.640 --> 00:38:21.400
 that's like a little more, you know what I mean?

00:38:21.400 --> 00:38:23.080
 And so like in the case of Kobe,

00:38:23.080 --> 00:38:25.640
 day one was just like, where is the crash?

00:38:25.640 --> 00:38:27.360
 What kind of helicopter was it?

00:38:27.360 --> 00:38:28.200
 You know what I mean?

00:38:28.200 --> 00:38:30.160
 The basic facts and can you visually do that?

00:38:30.160 --> 00:38:33.920
 Day two was, well let's get the play-by-play.

00:38:33.920 --> 00:38:35.160
 What was the like?

00:38:35.160 --> 00:38:36.200
 When did they take off?

00:38:36.200 --> 00:38:37.040
 Where did they go?

00:38:37.040 --> 00:38:39.120
 But you know, so we had a graphic

00:38:39.120 --> 00:38:41.640
 with the day two story that had that stuff.

00:38:41.640 --> 00:38:43.520
 Was there a route, a typical route?

00:38:43.520 --> 00:38:45.320
 Was it an unusual route, you know?

00:38:45.320 --> 00:38:49.200
 And then what happens typically in that is

00:38:49.200 --> 00:38:50.920
 after you learn the basic facts,

00:38:50.920 --> 00:38:52.160
 you kind of get to a point where you're like,

00:38:52.160 --> 00:38:54.600
 okay, here's what we think the story is.

00:38:54.600 --> 00:38:55.440
 You know what I mean?

00:38:55.440 --> 00:38:56.960
 So like within these facts,

00:38:56.960 --> 00:39:00.000
 here's what we think is worth sort of teasing out

00:39:00.000 --> 00:39:01.200
 visually for people.

00:39:01.200 --> 00:39:03.600
 So both has to be something insightful

00:39:03.600 --> 00:39:05.880
 that goes beyond the basic facts for people,

00:39:05.880 --> 00:39:09.200
 but it also has to have like a visual component to it, right?

00:39:09.200 --> 00:39:10.400
 And so in the case of Kobe,

00:39:10.400 --> 00:39:13.600
 our decision was to focus on the final turns of the helicopter.

00:39:13.600 --> 00:39:15.000
 All this is of course debatable,

00:39:15.000 --> 00:39:16.840
 but those were sort of our judgments we made

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:18.480
 like in those moments.

00:39:18.480 --> 00:39:20.280
 Like the conception boat crash,

00:39:20.280 --> 00:39:22.880
 the fire off Santa Barbara coast,

00:39:22.880 --> 00:39:24.980
 I guess a year ago now,

00:39:24.980 --> 00:39:27.480
 was another example of that where day one,

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:30.640
 it's just, well, where was the boat? What was the boats route?

00:39:30.640 --> 00:39:33.040
 Day two, or there are like advanced,

00:39:33.040 --> 00:39:34.560
 our piece for the weekend then was like,

00:39:34.560 --> 00:39:37.080
 well, what were the escape routes on this boat?

00:39:37.080 --> 00:39:39.880
 Where that was what seemed to us to be like the story.

00:39:39.880 --> 00:39:42.480
 You're kind of betting that that's how it's gonna shake out.

00:39:42.480 --> 00:39:43.320
 You know what I mean?

00:39:43.320 --> 00:39:44.680
 As the facts come in.

00:39:44.680 --> 00:39:46.760
 And then let's make a 3D model of the boats

00:39:46.760 --> 00:39:49.360
 so we can show people what these escape routes were like

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:51.400
 and emphasize that they weren't very good.

00:39:51.400 --> 00:39:53.120
 You know, it's what it ended up being.

00:39:53.120 --> 00:39:55.400
 So that's like a breaking new situation.

00:39:55.400 --> 00:39:57.080
 And then there's kind of in between ones

00:39:57.080 --> 00:40:00.400
 where it's like there's gonna be this long storyline.

00:40:00.400 --> 00:40:02.840
 And this is something that we're just trying to really

00:40:02.840 --> 00:40:04.800
 get better at in our department this year.

00:40:04.800 --> 00:40:07.240
 And is what I really admire about the New York Times

00:40:07.240 --> 00:40:09.560
 and some of the more mature departments,

00:40:09.560 --> 00:40:12.200
 is it seems to me that they have kind of decided on

00:40:12.200 --> 00:40:15.560
 for the year, here's what their major storylines are gonna be.

00:40:15.560 --> 00:40:17.960
 And then they come up with a string of visual coverage

00:40:17.960 --> 00:40:20.320
 to try to attack those major storylines.

00:40:20.320 --> 00:40:23.480
 And sometimes those can be planned like the election.

00:40:23.480 --> 00:40:25.960
 And other times like COVID, they just occur

00:40:25.960 --> 00:40:28.440
 and then you have to be smart pivot, right?

00:40:28.440 --> 00:40:30.240
 And so for us, we've done that this year

00:40:30.240 --> 00:40:33.840
 for the Floyd protests and for the COVID coverage

00:40:33.840 --> 00:40:38.440
 where we put together our own independent visual coverage plan

00:40:38.440 --> 00:40:39.720
 of like here's ideas.

00:40:39.720 --> 00:40:41.760
 Everybody in the team pitches things in.

00:40:41.760 --> 00:40:43.960
 We think about are there applications we could do?

00:40:43.960 --> 00:40:46.520
 We think about are there visual stories we could do?

00:40:46.520 --> 00:40:48.200
 And then we try to make them happen.

00:40:50.080 --> 00:40:51.720
 - Yeah, that's wonderful.

00:40:51.720 --> 00:40:52.840
 I just wanna jump in.

00:40:52.840 --> 00:40:54.240
 There's a really, really great question.

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:56.760
 I think it can kind of build on what you were,

00:40:56.760 --> 00:40:58.600
 I mean, what you just talked about.

00:40:58.600 --> 00:41:01.640
 But the question is about just sort of thought process

00:41:01.640 --> 00:41:03.440
 and developing stories and data,

00:41:03.440 --> 00:41:08.440
 but specifically about dealing with biases and storytelling.

00:41:08.680 --> 00:41:10.920
 And then the follow up to it is,

00:41:10.920 --> 00:41:13.480
 are there any specific examples that you could share

00:41:13.480 --> 00:41:18.160
 where you did face pushback on a story

00:41:18.160 --> 00:41:19.440
 that is sort of data driven,

00:41:19.440 --> 00:41:22.280
 but maybe there's some sort of part of it

00:41:22.280 --> 00:41:25.600
 that kind of, you know, there was a stop to it.

00:41:26.720 --> 00:41:28.520
 - Like internally at the LA Times, that's-

00:41:28.520 --> 00:41:29.840
 - Yeah.

00:41:29.840 --> 00:41:34.040
 - Well, you know, or maybe just from you.

00:41:34.040 --> 00:41:35.400
 Maybe you kind of, yeah.

00:41:35.400 --> 00:41:38.040
 - I think I have an example, it might not be exactly that.

00:41:38.040 --> 00:41:39.960
 I will just say I've been here 13 years

00:41:39.960 --> 00:41:42.600
 and you know, like any institution,

00:41:42.600 --> 00:41:45.280
 people have disagreements and conflicts and struggles,

00:41:45.280 --> 00:41:46.120
 you know what I mean?

00:41:46.120 --> 00:41:47.400
 And I've had my own.

00:41:47.400 --> 00:41:48.800
 But I will say that in that time,

00:41:48.800 --> 00:41:51.480
 I have never once felt that if I had a good story

00:41:51.480 --> 00:41:53.520
 that I knew was true, we couldn't get it published.

00:41:53.520 --> 00:41:54.600
 That's never happened.

00:41:55.640 --> 00:41:57.400
 There are people I really disagree with

00:41:57.400 --> 00:41:59.000
 and to be honest with you, I don't like

00:41:59.000 --> 00:41:59.960
 and they might not like me,

00:41:59.960 --> 00:42:04.400
 but I have at different times been able to come to them

00:42:04.400 --> 00:42:06.920
 with we have the story, check this out, let's do it.

00:42:06.920 --> 00:42:09.800
 And those people, they rise above all the personal stuff

00:42:09.800 --> 00:42:10.840
 and they're like, yeah, let's do it.

00:42:10.840 --> 00:42:11.680
 You know what I mean?

00:42:11.680 --> 00:42:13.600
 And to me, that's one of the great things about a newsroom

00:42:13.600 --> 00:42:17.040
 is that people who disagree or who are different

00:42:17.040 --> 00:42:19.160
 can unite around like a good story.

00:42:19.160 --> 00:42:21.200
 And like I have nothing but good things to say

00:42:21.200 --> 00:42:23.880
 about that personally, but like this is an example though

00:42:23.880 --> 00:42:26.840
 and of something slightly different but related,

00:42:26.840 --> 00:42:30.320
 which is how and one thing I really love about statistics

00:42:30.320 --> 00:42:33.120
 is how it can sometimes they can sometimes lead you

00:42:33.120 --> 00:42:36.120
 to conclusions that you do you would never imagine,

00:42:36.120 --> 00:42:36.960
 you know what I mean?

00:42:36.960 --> 00:42:40.080
 Or maybe we're counter to your initial bias

00:42:40.080 --> 00:42:41.760
 and then you end up telling a different story

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:43.320
 than the one you thought you were gonna tell

00:42:43.320 --> 00:42:45.200
 when it started out, right?

00:42:45.200 --> 00:42:50.080
 And so you guys remember Hurricane Harvey in Houston.

00:42:50.080 --> 00:42:51.960
 Oh, I have to send a Slack message really quick.

00:42:51.960 --> 00:42:52.720
 I have to,

00:43:02.120 --> 00:43:03.320
 this will just take a second.

00:43:03.320 --> 00:43:05.120
 I have bought a mechanical keyboard

00:43:05.120 --> 00:43:07.240
 during this like period at home guys

00:43:07.240 --> 00:43:10.040
 and it is so loud when I type that this might be deafening

00:43:10.040 --> 00:43:13.080
 but you guys remember Hurricane Harvey.

00:43:13.080 --> 00:43:15.440
 This was in Houston a couple years ago.

00:43:15.440 --> 00:43:18.280
 They had kind of like a big storm come through

00:43:18.280 --> 00:43:19.600
 and kind of flood the whole place

00:43:19.600 --> 00:43:22.920
 and it led to these sort of crazy images on television

00:43:22.920 --> 00:43:24.640
 of people trying to cross the city

00:43:24.640 --> 00:43:27.400
 in this sort of biblical flood kind of situation.

00:43:27.400 --> 00:43:28.840
 And it was a Friday night and we were sitting

00:43:28.840 --> 00:43:30.760
 in the newsroom at the LA Times

00:43:30.760 --> 00:43:33.640
 and an editor said to me, well, we gotta do something.

00:43:33.640 --> 00:43:34.480
 Let's come up with something.

00:43:34.480 --> 00:43:35.680
 It was one of these moments, right?

00:43:35.680 --> 00:43:37.320
 Like what are we gonna do?

00:43:37.320 --> 00:43:38.960
 This nuts thing is happening.

00:43:38.960 --> 00:43:41.080
 We have to come up with a way to cover it.

00:43:42.840 --> 00:43:45.240
 And like a lot of those situations,

00:43:45.240 --> 00:43:47.920
 you kind of start with what you're seeing in the news,

00:43:47.920 --> 00:43:50.760
 what you hear from other people and your own biases.

00:43:50.760 --> 00:43:51.600
 You know what I mean?

00:43:51.600 --> 00:43:53.000
 Like what you kind of think about the world.

00:43:53.000 --> 00:43:56.280
 And like, and then we're trying to come up

00:43:56.280 --> 00:43:57.120
 with a data story.

00:43:57.120 --> 00:43:58.920
 And so our initial thought was, hey,

00:43:58.920 --> 00:44:03.920
 if we get the data about where the FEMA flood zones are,

00:44:05.200 --> 00:44:07.840
 these officially defined flood zones from the government,

00:44:07.840 --> 00:44:08.680
 right?

00:44:08.680 --> 00:44:11.160
 And we compare it to the early damage data

00:44:11.160 --> 00:44:12.800
 that we see coming in from the government

00:44:12.800 --> 00:44:14.480
 about where buildings are damaged.

00:44:14.480 --> 00:44:17.280
 We're gonna find that all the people in these zones

00:44:17.280 --> 00:44:19.760
 got wiped out and we're gonna be able to add them up

00:44:19.760 --> 00:44:22.160
 into some big number and try to do a story

00:44:22.160 --> 00:44:25.080
 about the like extent of the damage, right?

00:44:25.080 --> 00:44:27.360
 And like, we didn't know exactly what it was gonna be,

00:44:27.360 --> 00:44:30.120
 but that was kind of our methodological bias

00:44:30.120 --> 00:44:32.640
 or frame that we like came to it with, right?

00:44:36.760 --> 00:44:38.800
 Let me just send this message, sorry.

00:44:38.800 --> 00:44:42.240
 And we're all fired up to do that.

00:44:42.240 --> 00:44:44.240
 I stayed up to like one in the morning, like it's like,

00:44:44.240 --> 00:44:47.480
 oh my gosh, the Harris County Property Assessors Office

00:44:47.480 --> 00:44:49.040
 has all the data online.

00:44:49.040 --> 00:44:50.040
 Yeah, grab it.

00:44:50.040 --> 00:44:52.520
 And the FEMA is having a conference call

00:44:52.520 --> 00:44:54.640
 and they're gonna give out the damage data.

00:44:54.640 --> 00:44:56.560
 Wow, there's property values.

00:44:56.560 --> 00:44:57.400
 Here's the flood zones.

00:44:57.400 --> 00:44:59.360
 I'm gonna put them all together and like find it out.

00:44:59.360 --> 00:45:02.000
 And like, and then we pitched that story to an editor

00:45:02.000 --> 00:45:03.440
 and we say, this is what story is gonna be.

00:45:03.440 --> 00:45:05.320
 It's gonna be about how screwed up Houston got

00:45:05.320 --> 00:45:06.600
 'cause all this.

00:45:06.600 --> 00:45:09.000
 And then I did the analysis

00:45:09.000 --> 00:45:12.520
 and what I quickly found, or not quickly, not so quickly,

00:45:12.520 --> 00:45:15.680
 what I soon found was that most of the damage

00:45:15.680 --> 00:45:19.960
 was actually outside of the official FEMA flood zones, right?

00:45:19.960 --> 00:45:21.840
 So that was an interesting thing.

00:45:21.840 --> 00:45:24.080
 And then we're like, okay, so let's start calling people

00:45:24.080 --> 00:45:25.000
 and let's figure out what's going on.

00:45:25.000 --> 00:45:27.520
 So we're just getting on the phone calling, texting

00:45:27.520 --> 00:45:28.760
 'cause we have all these addresses

00:45:28.760 --> 00:45:29.920
 from the property assessors.

00:45:29.920 --> 00:45:32.120
 Just call every person and just see what's going on.

00:45:32.120 --> 00:45:34.920
 And we did dozens and dozens of phone calls.

00:45:34.920 --> 00:45:38.320
 And what we found is that people inside

00:45:38.320 --> 00:45:42.040
 the official FEMA risk zones were actually like fine.

00:45:42.040 --> 00:45:44.760
 Like every one of them we could try to was like,

00:45:44.760 --> 00:45:45.600
 no, I'm good.

00:45:45.600 --> 00:45:46.440
 We were high.

00:45:46.440 --> 00:45:49.800
 We had put up like, we had elevated our house

00:45:49.800 --> 00:45:52.280
 or we had put in a retaining pond

00:45:52.280 --> 00:45:55.320
 and that caught all the water or our roads got redone.

00:45:55.320 --> 00:45:57.280
 And it was the people outside who had the problems.

00:45:57.280 --> 00:46:00.800
 And what we kind of pieced together then

00:46:00.800 --> 00:46:03.240
 from talking to experts and more and more people

00:46:03.240 --> 00:46:06.800
 was that our initial assumption was actually reverse.

00:46:06.800 --> 00:46:08.760
 And that's because believe it or not,

00:46:08.760 --> 00:46:11.120
 public policy works guys or it can.

00:46:11.120 --> 00:46:14.360
 It's that if you're inside an official FEMA flood zone,

00:46:14.360 --> 00:46:16.720
 there were all these additional building requirements

00:46:16.720 --> 00:46:19.320
 and retrofitting requirements for like taking care

00:46:19.320 --> 00:46:20.160
 and getting ready.

00:46:20.160 --> 00:46:22.120
 And so the people who lived in the quote unquote

00:46:22.120 --> 00:46:26.200
 riskiest areas actually ended up having the best outcomes

00:46:26.200 --> 00:46:29.040
 because they had to take all these preventive measures.

00:46:29.040 --> 00:46:30.800
 And so we ended up writing a story about that,

00:46:30.800 --> 00:46:33.200
 about how, hey, these regulations actually work,

00:46:33.200 --> 00:46:34.920
 but they're not nearly widespread enough

00:46:34.920 --> 00:46:37.160
 to save all these other people, you know what I mean?

00:46:37.160 --> 00:46:39.120
 It's kind of what the story ended up being.

00:46:39.120 --> 00:46:40.760
 And we had to kind of go back to the editor

00:46:40.760 --> 00:46:43.440
 and be like, oh, our initial pitch, it's like not that at all.

00:46:43.440 --> 00:46:47.240
 And that was this, it's a little hard to summarize,

00:46:47.240 --> 00:46:50.160
 but that was this sort of like weird process

00:46:50.160 --> 00:46:53.000
 of gathering numbers, listening to them,

00:46:53.000 --> 00:46:55.440
 talking to people on the other end of the data

00:46:55.440 --> 00:46:57.880
 and gradually having like a light bulb go on like,

00:46:57.880 --> 00:47:00.200
 oh, what everything I think is wrong.

00:47:00.200 --> 00:47:02.280
 And then you have, there's, whenever that happens,

00:47:02.280 --> 00:47:03.800
 there's always this like fun meeting

00:47:03.800 --> 00:47:05.520
 like in the middle of it where like you get together

00:47:05.520 --> 00:47:07.800
 and you're like, hey, we're totally wrong, right?

00:47:07.800 --> 00:47:09.560
 (laughing)

00:47:09.560 --> 00:47:11.560
 And you have to, okay, well, we can't just give up.

00:47:11.560 --> 00:47:13.240
 We gotta come up with like some story.

00:47:13.240 --> 00:47:15.440
 And so then oftentimes it's a more interesting story,

00:47:15.440 --> 00:47:16.280
 you know what I mean?

00:47:16.280 --> 00:47:19.120
 Because it's something you didn't expect, you know?

00:47:21.200 --> 00:47:24.240
 - Yeah, that's, yeah, that is actually amazing.

00:47:24.240 --> 00:47:26.640
 And it's really, I think it's just sort of really helpful

00:47:26.640 --> 00:47:31.040
 to kind of get that perspective on how you develop

00:47:31.040 --> 00:47:33.640
 and really work through these stories.

00:47:33.640 --> 00:47:37.360
 Another question, and I know we wanna be really sensitive

00:47:37.360 --> 00:47:40.400
 of having you here for too, too long,

00:47:40.400 --> 00:47:42.640
 but we have a couple of really good questions.

00:47:42.640 --> 00:47:45.840
 One, I think that would maybe really also be helpful

00:47:45.840 --> 00:47:48.480
 for students is just sort of thinking about

00:47:48.480 --> 00:47:51.920
 that tipping point between when you're creating a story,

00:47:51.920 --> 00:47:55.560
 when is it interactive and when is it static

00:47:55.560 --> 00:47:58.920
 and kind of like, how does that decision process get made

00:47:58.920 --> 00:48:01.760
 and how does that sort of, how does that happen?

00:48:01.760 --> 00:48:04.640
 - That's a tough one, and there's not one answer,

00:48:04.640 --> 00:48:08.360
 but I mean, if the question is when is something interactive?

00:48:08.360 --> 00:48:09.920
 Look, if you ask it that way,

00:48:09.920 --> 00:48:13.440
 the answer is less and less so every month and year.

00:48:13.440 --> 00:48:16.240
 And that has a lot to do with the changes we've seen

00:48:16.240 --> 00:48:19.560
 in consumer technology over the last five or 10 years.

00:48:19.560 --> 00:48:22.560
 As more and more people have moved to mobile phones.

00:48:22.560 --> 00:48:24.240
 Believe it or not, you probably will believe it.

00:48:24.240 --> 00:48:27.080
 You guys are into tech.

00:48:27.080 --> 00:48:30.080
 The majority of people who read the LA Times online

00:48:30.080 --> 00:48:33.400
 read it on a phone, like well more than 50%.

00:48:33.400 --> 00:48:36.200
 And so in a circumstance where the majority

00:48:36.200 --> 00:48:39.200
 of your audience is visiting you on a very small screen,

00:48:40.760 --> 00:48:44.160
 which they mostly just like thumb up and down on, right?

00:48:45.040 --> 00:48:48.240
 A lot of the interactive graphics that were very intricate

00:48:48.240 --> 00:48:51.680
 and made in Adobe Flash or whatever, 10 years ago,

00:48:51.680 --> 00:48:54.400
 really no longer makes sense because most people

00:48:54.400 --> 00:48:56.240
 aren't gonna interact with them and use them.

00:48:56.240 --> 00:48:58.920
 And the reality is, is what we learned from web analytics,

00:48:58.920 --> 00:49:00.400
 even before the mobile changes,

00:49:00.400 --> 00:49:03.320
 that most people never interacted with them anyway.

00:49:03.320 --> 00:49:06.880
 And so that's why you've seen a shift in,

00:49:06.880 --> 00:49:08.560
 if you follow kind of our little niche,

00:49:08.560 --> 00:49:11.600
 you've seen a shift away from most interactive graphics.

00:49:11.600 --> 00:49:14.280
 And so for the most part, we're looking for graphics

00:49:14.280 --> 00:49:19.280
 that can work static and small on mobile

00:49:19.520 --> 00:49:22.000
 and the way to make them more insightful

00:49:22.000 --> 00:49:24.760
 is usually through adding an annotation layer,

00:49:24.760 --> 00:49:27.080
 so like a little swoopy arrow or something.

00:49:28.040 --> 00:49:30.560
 Or a scrolling change, like you see

00:49:30.560 --> 00:49:33.760
 in that scrolling technique or the chart sort of changes

00:49:33.760 --> 00:49:36.240
 as the user continues to read the page.

00:49:36.240 --> 00:49:38.720
 Where interactivity still makes a lot of sense,

00:49:38.720 --> 00:49:43.080
 I think is in cases of personalization or of discovery.

00:49:43.080 --> 00:49:47.960
 So we're gonna have a chart that shows you

00:49:47.960 --> 00:49:51.000
 the total number of deaths at nursing homes from COVID.

00:49:51.000 --> 00:49:52.760
 But then when we have the, we're gonna have a table

00:49:52.760 --> 00:49:55.400
 that lets you look up the nursing home and care about, right?

00:49:55.400 --> 00:49:57.440
 And then that table should have like a search

00:49:57.440 --> 00:50:00.240
 and a filter and a way for you to like dig into it.

00:50:00.240 --> 00:50:02.440
 And then there's something like the live wildfires map,

00:50:02.440 --> 00:50:05.640
 which is really about one, giving you an overview

00:50:05.640 --> 00:50:08.000
 of like how many fires are there right now.

00:50:08.000 --> 00:50:09.160
 But then two,

00:50:11.400 --> 00:50:14.840
 two, letting you drill down into areas that you care about

00:50:14.840 --> 00:50:17.400
 to see kind of the latest data within those areas.

00:50:17.400 --> 00:50:20.640
 And so that's the map being there for personalization

00:50:20.640 --> 00:50:22.080
 and discovery, you know.

00:50:23.560 --> 00:50:25.960
 I think a lot of the interactivity that was just

00:50:25.960 --> 00:50:28.200
 like about fiddling, you've probably noticed

00:50:28.200 --> 00:50:29.480
 has like mostly disappeared.

00:50:29.480 --> 00:50:32.040
 Plus it's a lot, it takes more time to code.

00:50:32.040 --> 00:50:34.200
 - Yeah, very cool.

00:50:34.200 --> 00:50:36.800
 You know, we definitely wanna ask you this one question

00:50:36.800 --> 00:50:38.720
 for sure, because we have so many students here

00:50:38.720 --> 00:50:41.600
 who are interested in data and storytelling.

00:50:41.600 --> 00:50:44.440
 And sort of what is like, if you had one piece of advice

00:50:44.440 --> 00:50:47.960
 for students going out, working on school projects,

00:50:47.960 --> 00:50:49.880
 or really just wanting to break into

00:50:49.880 --> 00:50:54.160
 Ellie Times newsroom or other places, what is it?

00:50:54.160 --> 00:50:55.840
 What should they know?

00:50:55.840 --> 00:50:57.680
 - One piece of advice, I mean, I don't know.

00:50:57.680 --> 00:50:58.520
 - Or two or three.

00:50:58.520 --> 00:50:59.360
 - I can give you a call.

00:50:59.360 --> 00:51:00.680
 - Yeah, please.

00:51:00.680 --> 00:51:03.520
 - I mean, to me, this is a thing that has become cliche

00:51:03.520 --> 00:51:06.040
 and is said by some people who I think are kind of jerks,

00:51:06.040 --> 00:51:10.520
 but if you race them from your mind, it is true.

00:51:10.520 --> 00:51:11.800
 You gotta learn to code.

00:51:11.800 --> 00:51:13.960
 You don't have any, it's just like to really

00:51:13.960 --> 00:51:16.800
 do this well, you have to learn to code.

00:51:16.800 --> 00:51:19.120
 And you have to learn to code certain types of things,

00:51:19.120 --> 00:51:20.320
 not just like coding in general,

00:51:20.320 --> 00:51:22.160
 but I do think the fundamentals of programming

00:51:22.160 --> 00:51:23.320
 are so important.

00:51:24.160 --> 00:51:26.840
 The fundamentals of just like software and package design

00:51:26.840 --> 00:51:29.640
 are really underrated, just like how do I write a function

00:51:29.640 --> 00:51:32.480
 and then import it into another file and then reuse it

00:51:32.480 --> 00:51:34.560
 and like package code.

00:51:34.560 --> 00:51:38.320
 Like the basics of coding, I think are super important.

00:51:38.320 --> 00:51:40.880
 And then the sort of applied application of coding

00:51:40.880 --> 00:51:42.240
 to the types of things we do.

00:51:42.240 --> 00:51:44.440
 And so that, I think on the one hand,

00:51:44.440 --> 00:51:47.160
 is kind of data gathering and analysis.

00:51:47.160 --> 00:51:50.360
 So this is, how do I get data by scraping it

00:51:50.360 --> 00:51:53.720
 or reading it out of a database or building my own database?

00:51:53.720 --> 00:51:57.280
 And then how do I analyze it to like interview the data

00:51:57.280 --> 00:51:58.280
 is how we think about it.

00:51:58.280 --> 00:51:59.920
 What questions do I have for the data

00:51:59.920 --> 00:52:02.320
 and how can I write code that will help me get the answers

00:52:02.320 --> 00:52:03.720
 to those questions?

00:52:03.720 --> 00:52:06.600
 And so the tool that I teach a lot of classes on

00:52:06.600 --> 00:52:09.080
 that I like, but it's all debatable

00:52:09.080 --> 00:52:13.200
 is the Jupyter Notebook with Python data analysis tools.

00:52:13.200 --> 00:52:15.640
 They're free is kind of the best thing about it.

00:52:15.640 --> 00:52:18.320
 And the Jupyter environment once you figure out

00:52:18.320 --> 00:52:22.440
 how to get it running is just a really handy way

00:52:22.440 --> 00:52:25.160
 to do like step by step kind of data work.

00:52:25.160 --> 00:52:26.560
 And then on the other side,

00:52:26.560 --> 00:52:31.600
 there's the sort of how do we tell stories visually

00:52:31.600 --> 00:52:33.120
 through web development, right?

00:52:33.120 --> 00:52:39.080
 And that really involves learning fundamentals of HTML and CSS.

00:52:39.080 --> 00:52:42.040
 You know, again, just the basics are so important.

00:52:42.040 --> 00:52:43.520
 They're not even in HTML's case.

00:52:43.520 --> 00:52:46.600
 It's not even that hard, right?

00:52:46.600 --> 00:52:50.400
 And then how do you use JavaScript to make interactivity

00:52:50.400 --> 00:52:54.080
 and data visualizations?

00:52:54.080 --> 00:52:56.840
 I'm a big fan of D3.

00:52:56.840 --> 00:53:01.960
 And it's also a great way to get better at JavaScript, too,

00:53:01.960 --> 00:53:05.200
 because the D3 culture is really built around excellence

00:53:05.200 --> 00:53:06.920
 and programming, which I think is important.

00:53:06.920 --> 00:53:09.200
 I think there's a lot of free tools that are just about

00:53:09.200 --> 00:53:12.120
 cutting corners that don't really help you

00:53:12.120 --> 00:53:16.800
 over the long run become to master that type of thing.

00:53:16.800 --> 00:53:18.040
 So to me, there's the coding part,

00:53:18.040 --> 00:53:20.600
 but then there's the journalism part, which is thing two.

00:53:20.600 --> 00:53:24.040
 And this is where, like when I get applicants for internships

00:53:24.040 --> 00:53:26.840
 and stuff from data science programs,

00:53:26.840 --> 00:53:29.200
 which I'm always glad to get and I love to see,

00:53:29.200 --> 00:53:31.920
 you know, sometimes the people just haven't had the time

00:53:31.920 --> 00:53:34.520
 or opportunity to really apply their work in a way

00:53:34.520 --> 00:53:36.880
 that they're making something for the general public

00:53:36.880 --> 00:53:41.880
 or that has kind of a headline take away like point, you know?

00:53:41.880 --> 00:53:44.840
 A big part of editing and putting out a piece of journalism

00:53:44.840 --> 00:53:47.040
 is you kind of have to have like something to say.

00:53:47.040 --> 00:53:50.400
 And just writing a notebook that asks 10 questions

00:53:50.400 --> 00:53:52.560
 of the data or just making a graphic

00:53:52.560 --> 00:53:55.080
 that lets you fiddle with the numbers isn't enough, right?

00:53:55.080 --> 00:53:56.840
 The things that you're making need to kind of take

00:53:56.840 --> 00:54:01.320
 that next level of kind of journalistic whatever, you know,

00:54:01.320 --> 00:54:03.240
 by saying my finding is this.

00:54:03.240 --> 00:54:05.360
 And here's the headline that says it

00:54:05.360 --> 00:54:08.040
 and here's the three charts that show it, you know what I mean?

00:54:08.040 --> 00:54:10.720
 And you kind of like have a takeaway and a point

00:54:10.720 --> 00:54:12.400
 so that you have something to share.

00:54:12.400 --> 00:54:15.560
 And so when I see applicants who,

00:54:15.560 --> 00:54:17.160
 and I don't expect anyone who's being out

00:54:17.160 --> 00:54:18.840
 to really master any of this,

00:54:18.840 --> 00:54:22.440
 but when I see people who kind of have clearly put in the effort

00:54:22.440 --> 00:54:24.320
 to do both of those two things,

00:54:24.320 --> 00:54:26.800
 to like learn how to do the coding stuff to make stuff,

00:54:26.800 --> 00:54:28.400
 but also learn how to boil it down

00:54:28.400 --> 00:54:29.720
 and to make something out of it

00:54:29.720 --> 00:54:31.720
 for regular people, you know what I mean?

00:54:32.800 --> 00:54:35.000
 That's when I think you're a strong applicant

00:54:35.000 --> 00:54:39.160
 and you're sort of beginning the road to become do what we do.

00:54:39.160 --> 00:54:41.400
 But the reality is is learning how to code takes a while

00:54:41.400 --> 00:54:42.800
 and the things we're learning,

00:54:42.800 --> 00:54:45.160
 we're trying to do are emerging and experimental.

00:54:45.160 --> 00:54:48.920
 And so no one including myself really gets kind of good

00:54:48.920 --> 00:54:51.320
 at what we're doing until they've spent a couple of years

00:54:51.320 --> 00:54:52.760
 really apprenticing at it.

00:54:52.760 --> 00:54:55.680
 And that's just, that's just being human, you know?

00:54:57.480 --> 00:54:58.320
 - That's great.

00:54:58.320 --> 00:54:59.160
 That's really humbling.

00:54:59.160 --> 00:55:01.800
 And I think really honest to share that.

00:55:01.800 --> 00:55:04.720
 And I hope all the students out there

00:55:04.720 --> 00:55:06.080
 really get a lot out of that.

00:55:06.080 --> 00:55:07.960
 That's really helpful.

00:55:07.960 --> 00:55:10.800
 You know, I don't know if you wanted to kind of

00:55:10.800 --> 00:55:13.920
 have a wrap up question or...

00:55:13.920 --> 00:55:14.760
 - Yeah, whatever you guys got,

00:55:14.760 --> 00:55:17.160
 I'll take, I got a couple of minutes yet.

00:55:17.160 --> 00:55:18.320
 - Yeah, okay.

00:55:18.320 --> 00:55:22.080
 Well, well, first of all, thank you for kind of validating

00:55:22.080 --> 00:55:25.400
 this week for us from what you just said,

00:55:25.400 --> 00:55:29.000
 the value of coding, open data.

00:55:29.000 --> 00:55:31.480
 This is all that we've been kind of preaching

00:55:31.480 --> 00:55:32.880
 in the last couple of days.

00:55:33.960 --> 00:55:35.600
 Also should mention that if I look up

00:55:35.600 --> 00:55:37.480
 visual storyteller in the dictionary,

00:55:37.480 --> 00:55:39.480
 I think I'll find you in there.

00:55:40.680 --> 00:55:45.040
 You seem to embody that kind of visual storyteller mentality.

00:55:46.320 --> 00:55:50.960
 But I don't know if there's like one or two final questions.

00:55:50.960 --> 00:55:53.720
 Maybe somebody can unmute themselves

00:55:53.720 --> 00:55:57.880
 if you're out there and introduce yourself to Ben

00:55:57.880 --> 00:56:02.880
 and ask a question, hoping somebody can do that.

00:56:07.120 --> 00:56:09.720
 - I have a quick question.

00:56:09.720 --> 00:56:10.560
 - Please.

00:56:10.560 --> 00:56:13.360
 - And that's about, Ben, I see that you're active

00:56:13.360 --> 00:56:16.760
 in an organization called Data for Progress.

00:56:16.760 --> 00:56:19.240
 And I'm wondering if you'd talk about that a little bit.

00:56:19.240 --> 00:56:22.080
 It sort of ties in with what we've been doing

00:56:22.080 --> 00:56:24.600
 with humanitarian mapping.

00:56:24.600 --> 00:56:28.560
 And sort of how you see that kind of mission

00:56:28.560 --> 00:56:32.480
 converging with what you're doing at the LA Times,

00:56:32.480 --> 00:56:35.560
 which is obviously more of a commercial than--

00:56:35.560 --> 00:56:37.120
 - Yeah.

00:56:37.120 --> 00:56:39.080
 I don't know if I'm familiar with Data for Progress,

00:56:39.080 --> 00:56:40.640
 but I'm guessing it came up linked

00:56:40.640 --> 00:56:42.720
 to some other things that I do, you know what I mean?

00:56:42.720 --> 00:56:46.920
 But like for me personally, open source software

00:56:46.920 --> 00:56:49.160
 is probably as close as I get to religion.

00:56:49.160 --> 00:56:53.560
 Nice, but like, and I think is a really underrated

00:56:53.560 --> 00:56:56.200
 as a solution to a lot of problems.

00:56:56.200 --> 00:56:58.000
 It just needs more institutional support

00:56:58.000 --> 00:57:00.240
 from places like UCLA.

00:57:00.240 --> 00:57:03.680
 But what you guys are providing today, which is great,

00:57:03.680 --> 00:57:08.120
 you know, but like, and so I've been involved

00:57:08.120 --> 00:57:12.240
 in a number of kind of open source data gathering

00:57:12.240 --> 00:57:15.960
 and cleaning efforts related to helping journalists

00:57:15.960 --> 00:57:18.880
 both improve their skills, but also to kind of

00:57:18.880 --> 00:57:20.840
 build software and data infrastructure.

00:57:20.840 --> 00:57:23.320
 So we're not all like replicating each other's work

00:57:23.320 --> 00:57:27.120
 and we can benefit from kind of group collaboration.

00:57:27.120 --> 00:57:30.000
 One of those is called the California Civic Data Coalition.

00:57:30.000 --> 00:57:33.560
 And so this was a foundation funded open source

00:57:33.560 --> 00:57:37.960
 software project that refines and cleans up

00:57:37.960 --> 00:57:39.560
 the campaign finance data put up

00:57:39.560 --> 00:57:40.920
 by the California state government.

00:57:40.920 --> 00:57:42.680
 So this is the money in our state politics,

00:57:42.680 --> 00:57:44.320
 like all these Uber ads you're seeing

00:57:44.320 --> 00:57:46.280
 for Prop 22 right now.

00:57:46.280 --> 00:57:50.680
 And the data is there, it's just really difficult to access

00:57:50.680 --> 00:57:52.960
 because it's just in crappy shape.

00:57:52.960 --> 00:57:56.400
 And so there's kind of like this problem of who's going

00:57:56.400 --> 00:57:57.960
 to clean it up, right?

00:57:57.960 --> 00:57:59.920
 And so we created an open source group

00:57:59.920 --> 00:58:02.240
 to write the software that would be the refinery

00:58:02.240 --> 00:58:04.680
 for that like raw data, and then all the code

00:58:04.680 --> 00:58:07.360
 and all the effort is open source as well as the result.

00:58:07.360 --> 00:58:10.040
 Along the way we invented some bulk data loading

00:58:10.040 --> 00:58:12.160
 open source software tools, which people now use

00:58:12.160 --> 00:58:14.480
 for like totally different purposes, you know what I mean?

00:58:14.480 --> 00:58:17.040
 There's like side benefits to doing that.

00:58:17.040 --> 00:58:19.320
 And we're replicating that model currently

00:58:19.320 --> 00:58:21.240
 when it comes to tracking COVID data,

00:58:21.240 --> 00:58:23.520
 where initially the LA Times was doing 100%

00:58:23.520 --> 00:58:26.360
 of this COVID tracking ourselves in California,

00:58:26.360 --> 00:58:28.760
 but we now have a team of people from eight newsrooms

00:58:28.760 --> 00:58:31.600
 across the state who are helping us kind of gather it

00:58:31.600 --> 00:58:34.960
 and consolidate this cleaned up database of COVID data.

00:58:37.080 --> 00:58:41.000
 And I think that there's one, a lot of potential for that,

00:58:41.000 --> 00:58:43.400
 but two, I think as news organizations get smaller

00:58:43.400 --> 00:58:46.960
 and struggle, I don't know if there's a better alternative,

00:58:46.960 --> 00:58:47.800
 like who, you know what I mean?

00:58:47.800 --> 00:58:50.640
 Other than we just hope the New York Times gets big enough,

00:58:50.640 --> 00:58:52.240
 they can do it for everybody, you know what I mean?

00:58:52.240 --> 00:58:54.720
 Like that's really, the commercial solution

00:58:54.720 --> 00:58:57.040
 is going to be news monopoly, you know,

00:58:57.040 --> 00:58:59.480
 that's just big enough to do that is what I think.

00:58:59.480 --> 00:59:02.120
 But, you know, maybe I'm being too pessimistic.

00:59:02.120 --> 00:59:04.520
 So I think we need to band together

00:59:04.520 --> 00:59:06.120
 to come up with this alternative.

00:59:10.560 --> 00:59:12.040
 - Thank you.

00:59:12.040 --> 00:59:16.160
 Great, how about one last question for Ben?

00:59:16.160 --> 00:59:18.360
 How about one of the questions?

00:59:18.360 --> 00:59:19.200
 - I have a question.

00:59:19.200 --> 00:59:20.360
 - There we go, please.

00:59:22.360 --> 00:59:26.680
 - So I love your neighborhood map,

00:59:26.680 --> 00:59:29.920
 which is, I know, which has been on the page

00:59:29.920 --> 00:59:31.960
 for a while now, it's so great.

00:59:31.960 --> 00:59:36.320
 And I use it in my classes to talk to instruct students,

00:59:36.320 --> 00:59:38.200
 well, instruct students about two things,

00:59:38.200 --> 00:59:40.680
 one about the concept of place

00:59:40.680 --> 00:59:42.720
 and another about the concept

00:59:42.720 --> 00:59:45.000
 of volunteer geographic information.

00:59:46.600 --> 00:59:49.960
 And I'm curious if you guys have done other projects

00:59:49.960 --> 00:59:52.160
 that you think are within the realm

00:59:52.160 --> 00:59:54.920
 of like volunteer geographic information.

00:59:54.920 --> 00:59:57.080
 So like people actually map like-

00:59:57.080 --> 01:00:00.880
 - Yep, that project is one of my great white whales

01:00:00.880 --> 01:00:02.560
 and that we haven't really revived it.

01:00:02.560 --> 01:00:06.320
 There was a, the plan was to do it last year

01:00:06.320 --> 01:00:08.840
 for this census, but other things have come up.

01:00:11.680 --> 01:00:13.200
 And just so people know,

01:00:13.200 --> 01:00:16.240
 there is no official source of neighborhoods in LA County,

01:00:16.240 --> 01:00:18.560
 even though we all feel that we live in one.

01:00:19.640 --> 01:00:22.400
 And so about 10 years ago with some of my colleagues,

01:00:22.400 --> 01:00:25.160
 we sort of tried to pull the public

01:00:25.160 --> 01:00:28.320
 and then divide up LA County into, you know,

01:00:28.320 --> 01:00:30.000
 the LA Times neighborhoods at least.

01:00:30.000 --> 01:00:32.640
 And then we connected those kind of like

01:00:32.640 --> 01:00:34.960
 with the legos of census tracts.

01:00:34.960 --> 01:00:37.360
 So then all the areas have all this metadata

01:00:37.360 --> 01:00:40.600
 about demographics that are then merged with them,

01:00:40.600 --> 01:00:43.040
 which has allowed us to do dozens of investigative

01:00:43.040 --> 01:00:46.480
 and analytical stories to compare East LA to West LA,

01:00:46.480 --> 01:00:47.480
 you know what I mean?

01:00:48.560 --> 01:00:50.600
 It's not more in social science.

01:00:50.600 --> 01:00:53.480
 And one of my personal ambitions is for our team

01:00:53.480 --> 01:00:56.680
 to be more of a social science data provider.

01:00:56.680 --> 01:00:58.400
 We're trying to do that a little bit here with COVID,

01:00:58.400 --> 01:01:01.680
 but I would love to sort of be able to publish

01:01:01.680 --> 01:01:05.000
 sort of like the machine readable atlas

01:01:05.000 --> 01:01:07.480
 of LA demographics by neighborhood or something

01:01:07.480 --> 01:01:09.440
 so that we could see more and more people

01:01:09.440 --> 01:01:12.120
 able to do that type of analytical work.

01:01:12.120 --> 01:01:14.480
 But at the core of it was this crowdsourcing effort

01:01:14.480 --> 01:01:16.080
 'cause we didn't want to do it entirely alone.

01:01:16.080 --> 01:01:17.960
 So we sort of put out our own version of the maps

01:01:17.960 --> 01:01:20.400
 and then we had people tell us how wrong we were

01:01:20.400 --> 01:01:23.640
 and we modified them and you can't please everybody.

01:01:23.640 --> 01:01:24.680
 There's definitely flaws,

01:01:24.680 --> 01:01:27.000
 but that was kind of a collaborative process.

01:01:27.000 --> 01:01:28.440
 You know, that's the type of thing

01:01:28.440 --> 01:01:31.120
 that we haven't turned into like it.

01:01:31.120 --> 01:01:32.800
 We're going to do something like that every month,

01:01:32.800 --> 01:01:34.360
 but there's a few examples through the years

01:01:34.360 --> 01:01:35.800
 that I've had a lot of fun working on.

01:01:35.800 --> 01:01:40.280
 Like we finally, we're going to do the like a few years ago,

01:01:40.280 --> 01:01:43.920
 the like, what is the East Side story in the LA Times?

01:01:43.920 --> 01:01:44.760
 You know what I mean?

01:01:44.760 --> 01:01:46.680
 Where everybody brings out their grievances, you know,

01:01:46.680 --> 01:01:49.440
 and I will tell you as long as I am the data

01:01:49.440 --> 01:01:52.400
 and graphics editor, the East Side begins at the LA River.

01:01:52.400 --> 01:01:54.840
 I'm sorry, everybody who lives on Sunset,

01:01:54.840 --> 01:01:57.360
 you don't live on the East Side, sorry, you know what I mean?

01:01:57.360 --> 01:02:02.360
 But like, but we know people disagree, you know what I mean?

01:02:03.040 --> 01:02:04.040
 Like there is no answer.

01:02:04.040 --> 01:02:06.520
 And so we did like a one-off that was sort of

01:02:06.520 --> 01:02:08.080
 in that same tradition where it was,

01:02:08.080 --> 01:02:11.320
 here's a map of LA, draw the East Side, right?

01:02:11.320 --> 01:02:13.360
 And we had hundreds of people like draw them all.

01:02:13.360 --> 01:02:14.880
 And then we made this composite map

01:02:14.880 --> 01:02:18.280
 that was like everybody's East Side like overlaid.

01:02:18.280 --> 01:02:23.280
 And it was a little abstract and already, you know what I mean?

01:02:23.920 --> 01:02:26.360
 I don't know if it was like the clearest data visualization

01:02:26.360 --> 01:02:29.440
 ever drawn, but it sort of had this fun effort of like,

01:02:29.440 --> 01:02:32.400
 here's the splatter of like what everybody thinks, you know?

01:02:32.400 --> 01:02:34.840
 And though I'm sure our readership is biased

01:02:34.840 --> 01:02:36.760
 in a certain direction.

01:02:36.760 --> 01:02:41.760
 But so like that was an example of that, I guess.

01:02:41.760 --> 01:02:42.600
 - Yeah.

01:02:44.880 --> 01:02:47.040
 - You know, to me, the crowdsourcing thing that's out there

01:02:47.040 --> 01:02:48.680
 that nobody does much with Viz,

01:02:48.680 --> 01:02:49.920
 which we could do with Quakebot,

01:02:49.920 --> 01:02:53.160
 is the like, did you feel an earthquake data from like USGS?

01:02:53.160 --> 01:02:55.400
 I think it's like all of there for every earthquake

01:02:55.400 --> 01:02:58.000
 and like nobody really does much with it, you know?

01:02:59.440 --> 01:03:01.240
 I know the scientists at USGS do,

01:03:01.240 --> 01:03:03.960
 but like, I don't know if the news media does.

01:03:03.960 --> 01:03:04.800
 Yeah.

01:03:04.800 --> 01:03:06.120
 - Right.

01:03:06.120 --> 01:03:06.960
 Cool.

01:03:06.960 --> 01:03:07.800
 Thank you.

01:03:11.040 --> 01:03:11.880
 - All right.

01:03:11.880 --> 01:03:13.920
 Yeah, no, there's a whole bunch of chatter

01:03:13.920 --> 01:03:17.480
 about that project you're talking about mapping Los Angeles.

01:03:17.480 --> 01:03:18.320
 - Yeah.

01:03:18.320 --> 01:03:20.680
 - It's many of us fondly remember that kind of,

01:03:20.680 --> 01:03:22.960
 hey, we can contribute to defining boundaries,

01:03:22.960 --> 01:03:25.200
 which is not something we're used to.

01:03:25.200 --> 01:03:28.840
 Boundaries are usually defined by somebody up there.

01:03:28.840 --> 01:03:30.320
 And we abide by those boundaries.

01:03:30.320 --> 01:03:31.600
 And here's the LA Times saying,

01:03:31.600 --> 01:03:33.560
 hey, you define your own boundaries.

01:03:33.560 --> 01:03:35.760
 So that was a really neat project.

01:03:35.760 --> 01:03:39.440
 And still to this day in the urban planning department,

01:03:39.440 --> 01:03:43.720
 that's a go to resource for doing neighborhood level analysis.

01:03:43.720 --> 01:03:44.560
 - Yeah.

01:03:44.560 --> 01:03:46.160
 I was absolutely just going to say that.

01:03:46.160 --> 01:03:51.160
 I mean, I think the impact of that project in the classroom,

01:03:51.360 --> 01:03:53.480
 I just don't know if that kind of reaches you all,

01:03:53.480 --> 01:03:55.760
 but like, I mean, that is in so many different classes,

01:03:55.760 --> 01:03:58.280
 so many different students use that as sort of like,

01:03:58.280 --> 01:04:00.080
 understanding LA at this point.

01:04:00.080 --> 01:04:01.400
 - Yes, we have census tracts.

01:04:01.400 --> 01:04:02.680
 Yes, we have these other boundaries,

01:04:02.680 --> 01:04:06.040
 but those particular boundaries in that work

01:04:06.040 --> 01:04:09.080
 is just super, super influential in the classroom.

01:04:09.080 --> 01:04:09.920
 - Yeah.

01:04:09.920 --> 01:04:12.480
 I mean, to me, I just see all the times we fail to revive it.

01:04:12.480 --> 01:04:14.560
 So I feel a little guilty with that.

01:04:14.560 --> 01:04:17.400
 But like, you know, we kind of have, I have some ideas.

01:04:17.400 --> 01:04:20.120
 I might be like, this is one of these things

01:04:20.120 --> 01:04:23.200
 where like internally it gets caught up in the politics of like,

01:04:23.200 --> 01:04:25.920
 do we need a hyper local neighborhood news product?

01:04:25.920 --> 01:04:26.760
 You know what I mean?

01:04:26.760 --> 01:04:28.640
 And like, it's hard to have a conversation internally

01:04:28.640 --> 01:04:31.520
 without it being caught up with like traditional coverage.

01:04:31.520 --> 01:04:34.320
 It's my belief that it should be more of a data application

01:04:34.320 --> 01:04:37.720
 and like kind of like a social science hub

01:04:37.720 --> 01:04:40.160
 that is like easy enough for the average person

01:04:40.160 --> 01:04:41.520
 to understand, you know what I mean?

01:04:41.520 --> 01:04:44.480
 That's just like what is, you know,

01:04:44.480 --> 01:04:46.800
 Chevy bills or whatever, you know?

01:04:46.800 --> 01:04:50.440
 And I would like to kind of narrow kind of the mission

01:04:50.440 --> 01:04:53.280
 of it to really focus on that kind of popularizing

01:04:53.280 --> 01:04:54.800
 the demographics thing.

01:04:54.800 --> 01:04:57.960
 And so my hope is, is that we can do that

01:04:57.960 --> 01:04:59.880
 when the new census data comes out.

01:04:59.880 --> 01:05:03.040
 But as you know, it's the ACS is where the action really is.

01:05:03.040 --> 01:05:04.120
 So it doesn't matter.

01:05:06.240 --> 01:05:08.960
 I kind of wonder if we could partner that with like,

01:05:08.960 --> 01:05:10.160
 can I give you my pet idea?

01:05:10.160 --> 01:05:12.000
 And you guys can tell me if it's terrible.

01:05:13.600 --> 01:05:15.920
 With like a gentrification analysis.

01:05:15.920 --> 01:05:17.280
 - Oh, yeah.

01:05:17.280 --> 01:05:18.480
 - If you like that, yeah.

01:05:18.480 --> 01:05:19.560
 'Cause I did some reading

01:05:19.560 --> 01:05:20.880
 and you guys may know this better than me,

01:05:20.880 --> 01:05:22.720
 but there's these like academic definitions

01:05:22.720 --> 01:05:26.960
 of gentrification, which as far as I can tell,

01:05:26.960 --> 01:05:30.560
 are kind of limited to a sort of post-war redevelopment

01:05:30.560 --> 01:05:33.400
 sort of framework of like, how old is the housing stock?

01:05:33.400 --> 01:05:36.200
 And how recently has it been replenished?

01:05:36.200 --> 01:05:40.000
 And what is the change in housing value been, right?

01:05:40.000 --> 01:05:42.680
 And so like, you could, I look at those methods

01:05:42.680 --> 01:05:45.000
 and I could take these longitudinal census databases.

01:05:45.000 --> 01:05:46.560
 I can do that for every census tract

01:05:46.560 --> 01:05:48.680
 and then I could tell you what's the most gentrified neighborhood

01:05:48.680 --> 01:05:49.960
 in LA like that way, right?

01:05:49.960 --> 01:05:50.800
 That would be one way.

01:05:50.800 --> 01:05:53.600
 But like my hunch is that that's not enough.

01:05:53.600 --> 01:05:58.320
 Like in our 21st century life,

01:05:58.320 --> 01:06:01.680
 there's sort of this Brooklynized idea of gentrification,

01:06:01.680 --> 01:06:04.640
 which has to do with cultural products, right?

01:06:04.640 --> 01:06:07.960
 And commerce and like that kind of thing, you know what I mean?

01:06:07.960 --> 01:06:10.400
 Like what is the business in Highland Park now

01:06:10.400 --> 01:06:13.800
 on that strip on Fig versus 10 years ago, right?

01:06:13.800 --> 01:06:15.800
 Used to be a Latino typewriter shop

01:06:15.800 --> 01:06:19.840
 and now it's a hipster cocktail bar or whatever, you know?

01:06:19.840 --> 01:06:21.640
 And I think that's what people have in mind

01:06:21.640 --> 01:06:23.080
 when they send you an interpretation today.

01:06:23.080 --> 01:06:25.720
 And so like what I'm kind of reaching for

01:06:25.720 --> 01:06:27.760
 is could you come up with a metric

01:06:27.760 --> 01:06:29.560
 that would try to capture that

01:06:29.560 --> 01:06:31.320
 and then integrate it with the older metrics

01:06:31.320 --> 01:06:35.680
 into like a kind of more up-to-date gentrification index

01:06:35.680 --> 01:06:38.240
 or something, do anybody have any ideas

01:06:38.240 --> 01:06:40.840
 or have read anything that smiles ahead of me on this?

01:06:42.480 --> 01:06:44.720
 - Well, you know, there's a Center for Neighborhood Knowledge

01:06:44.720 --> 01:06:47.280
 here at UCLA that does a lot of research.

01:06:47.280 --> 01:06:48.600
 I don't know if you know Paul Wong,

01:06:48.600 --> 01:06:49.880
 he's a professor who--

01:06:49.880 --> 01:06:53.640
 - I don't know. - Wong worked with models

01:06:53.640 --> 01:06:56.680
 of gentrification and they have a site,

01:06:56.680 --> 01:06:59.320
 a project called Urban Displacement,

01:06:59.320 --> 01:07:01.880
 collaboration with Berkeley and UCLA

01:07:01.880 --> 01:07:04.920
 where they have index S for gentrification.

01:07:04.920 --> 01:07:05.760
 - No, really?

01:07:05.760 --> 01:07:08.880
 - It just talks about the need for us, you know,

01:07:08.880 --> 01:07:11.120
 in academia and also to let you know,

01:07:11.120 --> 01:07:14.640
 like UCLA is launching this kind of data acts initiative.

01:07:14.640 --> 01:07:17.280
 So it sounds like we are,

01:07:17.280 --> 01:07:21.280
 once that behind you guys, but we're right there

01:07:21.280 --> 01:07:25.160
 in terms of our efforts to think about,

01:07:25.160 --> 01:07:27.200
 like you said, you know, the importance of coding

01:07:27.200 --> 01:07:30.320
 in kind of the more social sciences,

01:07:30.320 --> 01:07:32.920
 which isn't traditionally been the case.

01:07:32.920 --> 01:07:36.680
 So just to think about how us in academia,

01:07:36.680 --> 01:07:39.240
 I mean, I feel such a stronger bond with you

01:07:39.240 --> 01:07:41.880
 through what we've been discussing today

01:07:41.880 --> 01:07:43.800
 and the importance that we work together

01:07:45.200 --> 01:07:49.000
 to create some meaningful stories

01:07:49.000 --> 01:07:53.680
 that can inform policy decisions across our communities.

01:07:55.320 --> 01:07:58.600
 - Yeah, I also love, I really love the idea

01:07:58.600 --> 01:08:01.880
 of kind of looking at sort of alternative data sources.

01:08:01.880 --> 01:08:06.160
 I was on a call yesterday and a workshop yesterday

01:08:06.160 --> 01:08:10.000
 actually looking at the Getty's new Riche project.

01:08:10.000 --> 01:08:12.920
 And they're putting all these images out there.

01:08:12.920 --> 01:08:15.480
 And this workshop was really trying to understand

01:08:15.480 --> 01:08:17.680
 how to look at those images, you know,

01:08:17.680 --> 01:08:20.560
 in this, you know, cultural production,

01:08:20.560 --> 01:08:22.200
 the streets that were photographed,

01:08:22.200 --> 01:08:25.720
 historically looking at, you know, racial change over time

01:08:25.720 --> 01:08:29.600
 and really talking about gentrification

01:08:29.600 --> 01:08:32.160
 and how a data set like that with those images

01:08:32.160 --> 01:08:35.000
 could be used with, you know, all these sources

01:08:35.000 --> 01:08:37.240
 that you're talking about, but other data sets

01:08:37.240 --> 01:08:40.440
 that are cultural and kind of trying to, you know,

01:08:40.440 --> 01:08:45.200
 look at that and also how to sort of code those images

01:08:45.200 --> 01:08:49.920
 and doing that coding so that it actually is, you know,

01:08:49.920 --> 01:08:51.600
 doing the work that we want it to do

01:08:51.600 --> 01:08:55.760
 in terms of understanding, you know, the displacement

01:08:55.760 --> 01:08:57.120
 or whatever's happening.

01:08:57.120 --> 01:08:59.160
 And not just sort of saying like, oh, you know,

01:08:59.160 --> 01:09:00.800
 this was here before and now it's gone,

01:09:00.800 --> 01:09:02.880
 but like, well, why is it gone?

01:09:02.880 --> 01:09:05.560
 And really kind of, you know, putting the point onto that.

01:09:05.560 --> 01:09:09.680
 So yeah, it's really interesting.

01:09:09.680 --> 01:09:10.520
 And I think you're right.

01:09:10.520 --> 01:09:11.960
 I mean, I think what we've been talking about

01:09:11.960 --> 01:09:15.480
 with, you know, really learning how to, you know,

01:09:15.480 --> 01:09:19.720
 build coding and, you know, also just stepping back

01:09:19.720 --> 01:09:22.560
 and being able to kind of piece, I think,

01:09:22.560 --> 01:09:25.120
 like really different data sets.

01:09:25.120 --> 01:09:27.360
 And the thing that we were talking about yesterday too

01:09:27.360 --> 01:09:30.600
 is just sort of understanding that world of data

01:09:30.600 --> 01:09:33.800
 and kind of, especially I think for things

01:09:33.800 --> 01:09:36.720
 like gentrification, kind of stepping out

01:09:36.720 --> 01:09:41.720
 and kind of looking at or trying to understand it

01:09:42.400 --> 01:09:47.400
 in a way that's local and trying to find sources

01:09:49.000 --> 01:09:53.240
 that maybe aren't that typical, you know, census,

01:09:53.240 --> 01:09:54.400
 you know, way of understanding it.

01:09:54.400 --> 01:09:55.960
 So I don't know, I love it.

01:09:55.960 --> 01:09:56.800
 Yeah.

01:09:56.800 --> 01:09:59.480
 - There's a guy at Harvard I found he did a paper read

01:09:59.480 --> 01:10:02.320
 used Yelp data and then he tried to like correlate

01:10:02.320 --> 01:10:05.000
 the arrival of certain types of businesses

01:10:05.000 --> 01:10:07.440
 with other gentrification indicators, you know,

01:10:07.440 --> 01:10:10.280
 like educated population or whatever.

01:10:10.280 --> 01:10:15.280
 And so there might be something there, you know,

01:10:15.960 --> 01:10:18.880
 but I kind of imagine the index.

01:10:18.880 --> 01:10:21.600
 You take like the traditional metric of like buildings

01:10:21.600 --> 01:10:25.160
 or whatever, you know, you add in the racial dynamic

01:10:25.160 --> 01:10:27.560
 of, you know, white invasion, as I've learned,

01:10:27.560 --> 01:10:29.080
 it's called in some papers.

01:10:29.080 --> 01:10:32.960
 And then you maybe add like a third cultural dimension

01:10:32.960 --> 01:10:35.480
 of these products and then you like mix those into like

01:10:35.480 --> 01:10:38.000
 a radar chart or like an index or something

01:10:38.000 --> 01:10:40.280
 and that'd be kind of cool.

01:10:40.280 --> 01:10:42.200
 My bet is what Adams would be number one.

01:10:42.200 --> 01:10:44.040
 That's my bet guys.

01:10:44.040 --> 01:10:45.720
 - I would say from personal experience

01:10:45.720 --> 01:10:49.560
 with Brooklyn and here in LA, the first major indicator

01:10:49.560 --> 01:10:51.840
 should be when the real estate agent decides

01:10:51.840 --> 01:10:53.840
 to change the name of the name.

01:10:53.840 --> 01:10:54.680
 - There you go.

01:10:54.680 --> 01:10:55.520
 That's another one.

01:10:55.520 --> 01:10:56.680
 - That's an indicator.

01:10:56.680 --> 01:10:58.520
 - One thing I learned looking at the literature too,

01:10:58.520 --> 01:11:01.160
 I thought was interesting is traditionally only like

01:11:01.160 --> 01:11:03.360
 the lowest quartile could ever be thought of

01:11:03.360 --> 01:11:04.480
 as having gentrified.

01:11:04.480 --> 01:11:08.080
 So you had to be depressed to ever undergo the process.

01:11:08.080 --> 01:11:10.080
 And I don't think that people think about it that way

01:11:10.080 --> 01:11:11.120
 today anymore.

01:11:11.120 --> 01:11:13.320
 Like, 'cause when I ran the traditional numbers,

01:11:13.320 --> 01:11:16.720
 I saw that Venice Beach had a really high score

01:11:16.720 --> 01:11:19.080
 on some of the values but wouldn't have been considered

01:11:19.080 --> 01:11:21.600
 because it was already a pretty wealthy place to begin with.

01:11:21.600 --> 01:11:22.440
 Right?

01:11:22.440 --> 01:11:23.920
 And so like there's this way in which I think

01:11:23.920 --> 01:11:27.760
 the traditional numeric framework doesn't mesh

01:11:27.760 --> 01:11:29.320
 with how people really think about it

01:11:29.320 --> 01:11:30.440
 or talk about it today.

01:11:30.440 --> 01:11:32.280
 And like, that's the thing I just feel like

01:11:32.280 --> 01:11:34.040
 there must be some way to wrestle that.

01:11:34.040 --> 01:11:34.880
 But.

01:11:36.800 --> 01:11:38.360
 - That could be a,

01:11:38.360 --> 01:11:40.720
 and this is a very geographically nerdy

01:11:40.720 --> 01:11:43.880
 but a modifiable aerial unit problem issue

01:11:43.880 --> 01:11:47.560
 where because you're looking at Venice as a whole.

01:11:47.560 --> 01:11:49.160
 - Right, we've predefined it.

01:11:49.160 --> 01:11:50.000
 - Yeah.

01:11:50.000 --> 01:11:51.880
 - Very wealthy parts get, you know,

01:11:51.880 --> 01:11:54.440
 averaged with the less wealthy parts.

01:11:54.440 --> 01:11:55.280
 So those.

